WEBVTT

02:18.160 --> 02:21.780
Good. And you're good? Okay. All right.

02:22.662 --> 02:25.834
We're going to go ahead and get started and call this meeting, our work session,

02:25.866 --> 02:29.359
to order. It's 4:01. And Ben is

02:29.375 --> 02:32.178
going to be here in just a minute. I think he's online for now until

02:32.678 --> 02:36.296
he gets here. Okay. And also just wanted to point out we do

02:36.360 --> 02:40.114
have a lot of closed session items to get through tonight

02:40.614 --> 02:44.223
for the council's awareness. So I want to stick as close as we

02:44.723 --> 02:48.396
can to these timelines, and we've told each of the presenters to make sure that

02:48.896 --> 02:52.360
half the time's for the presentation and half the time's for some

02:52.860 --> 02:55.747
discussion if that's the case. If there's something that requires more time,

02:56.341 --> 02:59.599
then just let us know that we needed to

02:59.872 --> 03:02.681
bring it back or something. But I want to try and make sure that we

03:02.745 --> 03:05.378
can stay on track because we got a lot to do in, in a minute.

03:05.426 --> 03:08.847
So. Thank you. With that in mind, I did move some things

03:09.347 --> 03:12.743
around earlier, but I don't think it changed anything.

03:12.792 --> 03:16.105
So we're gonna start off with 1D, discussion item 1D,

03:16.121 --> 03:20.345
which is the Well-Being Project survey results.

03:21.076 --> 03:23.852
So, Abby, were you gonna introduce?

03:25.532 --> 03:29.316
Yes, thank you, Mayor. I just wanna introduce

03:29.816 --> 03:32.907
Dr. Courtney Flint. She's a professor at Utah State University.

03:34.077 --> 03:35.854
They did a well-being survey that was conducted in April 2021.

03:37.470 --> 03:40.938
2026 of this year, and she's here to share the results, which I think are

03:41.438 --> 03:43.571
really interesting. So I'm gonna turn the time over to her. Thank you for doing

03:43.603 --> 03:46.991
this. Sure. Awesome. So great to be with you. It's great to

03:47.491 --> 03:50.475
be partners again. We partnered with you on this project in 2020.

03:50.893 --> 03:54.217
A lot's changed in Eagle Mountain since then, so it's fun to look

03:54.717 --> 03:58.632
at the results. I'll hop right in. So the goals of the project, you know,

03:58.761 --> 04:02.068
I'm from Utah State. We're the land-grant university. Any Aggies in the house?

04:03.416 --> 04:05.694
Oh, we're far from Aggieland. Okay.

04:07.105 --> 04:10.136
Well, it's great to be here. We really provide,

04:10.152 --> 04:13.856
with the Utah Well-Being Project, a way to help you keep your

04:14.356 --> 04:17.993
fingers on the pulse of how your residents are doing, inform your planning processes

04:18.891 --> 04:22.259
and decision-making. We do some research on the data related

04:22.759 --> 04:26.556
to well-being, and we support statewide and regional initiatives. We work closely with

04:26.605 --> 04:29.459
health districts, Department of Health, Department of Transportation,

04:29.876 --> 04:32.248
Office of Planning and Budget, things like that.

04:33.098 --> 04:35.679
As you can see from the map, we are all up and down the Wasatch

04:35.727 --> 04:38.885
Front and east and west and north and south across the

04:39.385 --> 04:43.245
state. Our city reports from our 2026 surveys are online.

04:43.341 --> 04:46.627
Yours is up on our website, so I encourage you to check that out if

04:47.127 --> 04:50.459
you haven't already. So this is an online

04:50.491 --> 04:53.825
survey. It is a no-cost partnership with you,

04:54.819 --> 04:58.409
emphasis on the word partnership. This would not happen without us

04:58.909 --> 05:02.177
working together because we provide the survey, We provide

05:02.677 --> 05:06.008
some materials, but you do the recruitment. We figure cities know best how to reach

05:06.104 --> 05:09.134
their residents, and we don't have the budget to do it any other way,

05:09.166 --> 05:12.628
so this is how it works. You did participate in 2020.

05:12.692 --> 05:16.876
You had 506 responses then, and you had 359

05:16.924 --> 05:20.531
responses. These are all pretty comparable numbers

05:21.349 --> 05:24.234
to other cities similar to you all.

05:25.581 --> 05:29.572
We like to be— since it's not a proportionate representative sampling

05:29.829 --> 05:33.051
effort, we like to be very transparent about who we

05:33.551 --> 05:36.952
are reaching, and who we are not. So this graph here compares

05:37.065 --> 05:40.855
the survey demographics with the demographics that we can see from recent

05:41.355 --> 05:44.790
census data. The closer the dots are, and really you can

05:44.854 --> 05:49.158
barely see that there are 2 dots in many of these places, what that means

05:49.415 --> 05:52.771
is the closer the dots are means we have really,

05:52.884 --> 05:56.626
really represented that particular demographic. So we've

05:56.642 --> 06:00.063
done actually really well according— you look across income,

06:00.352 --> 06:04.407
Most of the age categories here, employment and

06:04.600 --> 06:07.934
homeowners and renters, those dots are overlapping. I mean,

06:07.966 --> 06:11.605
that's just really incredible. We don't see that kind of correlation as well.

06:12.534 --> 06:15.885
We do overrepresent those who are married, but we're

06:16.385 --> 06:20.293
pretty darn close on most everything else. That 18 to

06:20.793 --> 06:23.050
29-year-old age group is hard to reach anyway.

06:24.156 --> 06:28.036
Mostly full-time residents, 50% have lived here for

06:28.148 --> 06:31.450
5 years or less. Super high when you compare it to a

06:31.950 --> 06:35.096
lot of our communities. Communities that are in the project. I should say there were

06:35.209 --> 06:39.613
57 other— 56 other communities that we are reporting

06:39.645 --> 06:43.042
on this spring. So let's dive into

06:43.542 --> 06:47.421
the results. So personal well-being in Eagle Mountain, we compare the

06:47.486 --> 06:50.704
group at the bottom are the rapid growth cities. We use the League of Cities

06:50.737 --> 06:52.239
and Towns caucus groups,

06:54.394 --> 06:56.385
and so that's what we've represented here.

06:59.133 --> 07:02.867
You may or may not sort of feel comfortable there, although you are certainly rapidly

07:02.899 --> 07:05.880
growing. Average of 3.92,

07:05.944 --> 07:09.309
72% said a 4 or a 5. It ranks a

07:09.373 --> 07:12.803
little bit low here in terms of comparing to other rapid growth

07:12.819 --> 07:16.937
cities, but you're right there with other nearby neighbors in Saratoga

07:17.437 --> 07:21.216
Springs and in Lehigh. You're all probably not even statistically significantly

07:21.248 --> 07:24.518
different from each other. If you look at

07:25.018 --> 07:27.743
the big scale and you think from all the way to the big cities to

07:28.243 --> 07:31.721
the really small towns, you can see that there are a fair few— there's

07:31.753 --> 07:35.588
some communities that are lower than Eagle Mountain. So don't get

07:36.088 --> 07:39.037
too hung up on your positionality there in those rapid growth cities. And I think

07:39.537 --> 07:42.951
we've got some good explanations for why that's the case where it is

07:43.063 --> 07:46.641
at the moment. Community well-being, we see it always

07:46.753 --> 07:50.571
declines for respondents. They often feel that others,

07:50.924 --> 07:53.587
the community as a whole, is not doing as well as they are personally.

07:54.453 --> 07:58.379
So we have the data here. 42% say

07:58.879 --> 08:02.032
a 4 or a 5. Change over time since 2020. Now, we don't

08:02.064 --> 08:05.189
know if it's the same group taking the survey or not, but if

08:05.689 --> 08:09.692
you look on average here, you can see that well-being has declined somewhat

08:10.192 --> 08:14.179
since our 2020 survey data. We look beyond

08:14.227 --> 08:18.233
these general categories and we dive into 12 different what we call domains

08:18.249 --> 08:21.742
or categories of well-being. And this has been informed

08:21.774 --> 08:25.187
by various federal and international agencies.

08:25.540 --> 08:29.049
You can see that here in this graph, it shows us that the most important

08:29.209 --> 08:32.750
well-being domains to the people of Eagle Mountain are mental

08:33.250 --> 08:36.339
health, safety and security, living standards, and family life.

08:37.157 --> 08:41.162
The highest-rated domains are family life, safety and security,

08:41.226 --> 08:44.495
and mental health. So really strong alignment with those things

08:44.527 --> 08:47.781
that matter most to folks are doing really well. So that is a that is

08:47.829 --> 08:51.633
important. The red zone, which is those

08:51.745 --> 08:55.324
things that are important to folks but rated a little lower than average,

08:55.597 --> 08:58.936
we find leisure time and local environmental quality.

08:59.193 --> 09:02.579
Now leisure time, we know that your largest

09:03.079 --> 09:06.239
age group is actually in that 30 to 39-year-old range,

09:06.656 --> 09:10.107
and that group, as we look across the state data, those people

09:10.607 --> 09:14.072
are not the— don't have the highest well-being. In fact, they're really quite low,

09:14.521 --> 09:18.118
and they're really, really busy. They're often raising families and working hard,

09:18.712 --> 09:22.180
and so leisure time is a sparse commodity for that group. So that

09:22.228 --> 09:26.371
may explain why that's there. Local environmental quality is the

09:26.885 --> 09:30.369
most prominent red zone category around the

09:30.869 --> 09:34.255
state. So we all, as a state, you're not alone in that position.

09:35.154 --> 09:37.836
And as we look at these things over time, you can see that some of

09:38.336 --> 09:42.380
them have declined ever so slightly. A couple more, education, local environmental

09:42.880 --> 09:46.571
quality, have that deeper dip. over those 6

09:47.071 --> 09:49.830
years. Safety and security has gone up just a tad.

09:51.227 --> 09:54.984
Another thing we measure with the wellbeing survey is community connection.

09:55.065 --> 09:58.260
How connected do you feel to Eagle Mountain as

09:58.308 --> 10:02.065
a community? And we see this is actually quite low

10:02.565 --> 10:05.902
here. But let's remember that 50% of the

10:06.402 --> 10:09.981
respondents had lived here less than 5 years or less. Well,

10:09.997 --> 10:13.626
there's not much time for people to get a strong sense of community

10:14.126 --> 10:17.560
like some of our very well-established communities that are also in the survey

10:18.363 --> 10:21.992
set this year. But it does give you some benchmarks

10:22.492 --> 10:25.252
of places you can go, and I have some data that I think will give

10:25.752 --> 10:29.331
you some, perhaps some pointers. We can see that community

10:29.347 --> 10:33.105
connection is very important here. We see a strong, pretty strong

10:33.137 --> 10:36.959
correlation with overall well-being. We see it with mental health.

10:37.408 --> 10:40.654
So, the stronger that sense of community connection, it does have

10:42.051 --> 10:45.457
a benefit in some of these other areas of life.

10:46.871 --> 10:50.936
There's a couple of things going on in terms of personal demographics and well-being.

10:51.369 --> 10:54.229
Those with disabilities have lower well-being scores.

10:54.599 --> 10:58.133
Those who are homeowners have a higher well-being score.

10:58.920 --> 11:02.615
And we do see a bit of that correlation with income. We see

11:02.631 --> 11:06.755
it very strongly across the data across the state. It is certainly a

11:06.804 --> 11:09.918
signature there in your data, but perhaps not as strong as others.

11:11.806 --> 11:15.220
Also, the community connection, those who are

11:15.365 --> 11:19.729
not Hispanic or Latino seem to have a lower

11:20.229 --> 11:25.245
sense of community connection here. That is really quite— yes,

11:25.358 --> 11:29.036
the Hispanic and Latino community has a higher sense of community connection, and that is

11:29.085 --> 11:31.198
the reverse of most of the communities in the survey.

11:32.286 --> 11:35.873
We also see by age, you can see those age groups

11:36.066 --> 11:40.040
in the from 50, 40 to 50 are actually feeling

11:40.152 --> 11:44.098
more community connected than those who are 18 to 39

11:44.276 --> 11:47.367
or those who are 60 years and over.

11:49.014 --> 11:52.518
Now here's one of the things we're finding quite interesting is well a

11:53.068 --> 11:56.297
Eagle Mountain residents are really very active. They're walking,

11:56.362 --> 11:59.332
they're biking, they're recreating in parks, they're gardening.

11:59.936 --> 12:03.676
They are using trails in and around your city, and everywhere you

12:03.724 --> 12:06.838
see a star, a big star there for community events,

12:06.918 --> 12:11.268
those who attend community events have a statistically

12:11.768 --> 12:14.960
significantly higher level of community connection. So those community

12:15.460 --> 12:18.812
events pay off for those who participate. And then all those smaller

12:19.312 --> 12:22.809
stars, those are still correlated, not quite as strongly as that one with

12:22.825 --> 12:26.565
community connection, but lots of correlations there with well-being

12:26.597 --> 12:29.809
and with community connection. And really, these things appear

12:30.309 --> 12:33.343
to be— the data are showing us that these appear to be worth the investment.

12:34.452 --> 12:38.805
Working on your parks, working on your trails, having these community events, having rec programs,

12:39.207 --> 12:43.046
those who participate seem to have these higher levels of connection and

12:43.546 --> 12:47.287
well-being. Okay, here's a striking one. Folks in Eagle

12:47.335 --> 12:50.950
Mountain feel that the current rate of population growth is too fast.

12:51.014 --> 12:54.709
It is not unlike some of the neighbors around here.

12:56.334 --> 12:59.388
So Saratoga Springs bumps you out just by a little bit,

12:59.404 --> 13:03.708
but yeah, y'all know about your 100% population

13:04.208 --> 13:08.162
growth rate, I'm sure. That percentage

13:08.227 --> 13:11.857
that's saying too fast has gone up in the 6 years since we did this

13:12.357 --> 13:15.773
survey previously, and those that thought it was just right, well, that's plummeted.

13:15.885 --> 13:19.202
So they've replaced, they've switched from just right to too

13:19.267 --> 13:22.809
fast. Economic development, you can

13:23.309 --> 13:25.234
see here, is a lot more mixed.

13:26.736 --> 13:30.376
We've got 50% of folks saying it's actually too

13:30.876 --> 13:34.304
slow, but we still have some saying just right or too fast.

13:35.716 --> 13:39.356
The too slow folks, about the same. They are exactly the same.

13:39.740 --> 13:42.819
The just right folks have declined a little bit, whereas the too fast

13:42.931 --> 13:46.331
folks have come up. So really a mixed kind of response

13:46.379 --> 13:49.971
here. One of the things we look at is cost of living

13:50.003 --> 13:53.787
this year. For those for whom childcare is relevant,

13:53.932 --> 13:57.353
and that looks to be about 46% of the survey takers,

13:57.932 --> 14:02.011
that 74% of them are saying that that is difficult or very

14:02.108 --> 14:06.027
difficult to afford. That one's jumping out in many cities and towns.

14:07.023 --> 14:10.878
But healthcare and housing are also right up there at nearly half of respondents

14:11.378 --> 14:14.830
are saying those are pretty hard to afford. And you still have just

14:14.894 --> 14:18.363
under a third or about a third saying that even food, utilities,

14:18.428 --> 14:21.528
and transportation is is a challenge. So cost of living,

14:21.817 --> 14:26.025
not unique to Eagle Mountain, but something that's going on. Barriers to travel,

14:26.057 --> 14:29.655
travel time, lack of routes and cost are really quite

14:30.155 --> 14:33.333
high here compared to most cities, even in the Wasatch Front.

14:33.381 --> 14:36.657
So you're standing out there a bit. We ask about a lot

14:37.157 --> 14:40.898
of different concerns. This list has grown a fair bit since you participated

14:41.398 --> 14:45.026
in 2020. You've got traffic at the top of

14:45.074 --> 14:47.902
your list. And water supply and water quality there.

14:48.705 --> 14:52.063
And so that's not uncommon to be on the top,

14:52.079 --> 14:55.903
but your percentages are really quite high. You've got a whole smattering

14:55.920 --> 14:59.454
of other things that are above 50% of folks have a moderate

14:59.954 --> 15:04.371
or major concern on these items. I just want to take one minor aside

15:04.871 --> 15:07.746
and say that my other hat is I am the Associate Director of the Utah

15:07.890 --> 15:11.329
Water Research Laboratory up at Utah State. Water is

15:11.747 --> 15:15.537
everything we do. We provide a great deal of services across the

15:16.037 --> 15:19.601
suite of water flows and supply through to water quality

15:19.633 --> 15:23.392
and help with wastewater treatment plants. If you folks

15:23.892 --> 15:27.440
in engineering and in public works have any interest in chatting with us

15:27.940 --> 15:31.488
about how we can support you in the water realms, especially given

15:31.520 --> 15:34.941
how concerned your residents are, we would be really happy to have that

15:34.973 --> 15:38.346
conversation. We would welcome that. Just 2 more

15:38.846 --> 15:42.873
slides. What do people value about Eagle Mountain? They really value— some

15:42.921 --> 15:46.357
of them clearly have a sense of community, feeling of community,

15:46.437 --> 15:49.937
people are friendly. They value the space out here,

15:50.050 --> 15:53.181
the quieter pace of life, and the family-oriented environment.

15:54.304 --> 15:57.451
They do feel there's some sense of peace here, at least somebody did,

15:57.837 --> 16:01.353
and that those trails, that sense of

16:01.385 --> 16:04.305
connection to nature is a valued out here.

16:05.059 --> 16:08.842
What would improve well-being? Focusing on more roads

16:09.342 --> 16:13.315
and infrastructure to accommodate the growth. Another road in and out

16:13.411 --> 16:16.408
they would certainly think would benefit them.

16:17.483 --> 16:20.833
There's some— many seeking a better balance between

16:21.009 --> 16:24.392
growth and homes. We see that in many

16:24.892 --> 16:28.512
of these rapid growth communities. A rec center for especially

16:28.624 --> 16:32.150
folks on the On the young age and the older age is something that

16:32.215 --> 16:34.517
we were hearing a lot in the open comments,

16:35.126 --> 16:38.596
access to recreation areas and making sure people

16:39.096 --> 16:42.662
know about those areas. And with that, I want to say thank you so

16:43.162 --> 16:46.075
much for being a partner. And if I can answer any quick questions, I would

16:46.575 --> 16:50.078
be happy to. I will also add quickly that

16:50.158 --> 16:53.335
I have also shared all of your open comments, and there are

16:53.835 --> 16:58.044
many of them. I think about 80% of the respondents

16:58.544 --> 17:00.874
They've got a lot on their mind. So you have those at your disposal.

17:00.954 --> 17:04.259
We do not share those publicly. We just share them with you.

17:04.403 --> 17:08.174
We use a couple here and there as part of research, but the

17:08.674 --> 17:11.880
bulk of them are yours. So one quick question. How statistically

17:12.380 --> 17:16.500
significant do you think the survey is? Well, because we

17:16.516 --> 17:20.014
allow any resident age 18 or older to participate, it is

17:20.126 --> 17:23.254
not based on a probability sampling. But we know

17:23.754 --> 17:27.824
that based on your adult population and the responses that we have here, you're looking

17:28.324 --> 17:31.577
at about a margin of error of about 5%. But we cannot

17:31.657 --> 17:35.120
say that it is scientifically significantly

17:35.620 --> 17:38.921
representative. However, the demographic correlations that

17:38.969 --> 17:43.026
we saw suggest that— and the numbers of respondents that you have—

17:43.298 --> 17:46.345
say that I would take these results quite seriously.

17:46.842 --> 17:50.199
I can't— because of the sampling lack of— This representative

17:50.699 --> 17:54.021
sampling, I'm not going to go all the way to the bank, but I'm going

17:54.521 --> 17:56.617
to say you've got some pretty good data here compared to many cities.

17:58.668 --> 18:01.840
I was just going to say thank you for that presentation. As I was going

18:01.872 --> 18:05.093
through it, I was like, yep, yep, yep. Like a lot of things make sense

18:05.593 --> 18:08.844
around traffic and connections and parks and all of

18:09.344 --> 18:12.993
that. What— you do this for many other

18:13.493 --> 18:17.942
cities. What were the, like, the Number 1, number 2 things that

18:18.442 --> 18:21.041
would come to the top of your list that you'd recommend our council look at?

18:21.715 --> 18:24.927
Well, I think working on that community connection score, I think,

18:24.991 --> 18:28.764
would be a top item. And that can vary for different folks, what would

18:28.845 --> 18:32.778
make them feel more connected. We could probably dig in here to

18:33.278 --> 18:36.809
your demographics a little bit more and give you more precision on

18:37.309 --> 18:39.602
that if you're interested in that. We would be glad to follow up.

18:40.502 --> 18:44.214
The community connection can be done through community events,

18:44.278 --> 18:47.974
but it can also be gathering places where people

18:48.474 --> 18:51.332
can just get to know each other a little bit better. And those can be

18:51.832 --> 18:54.578
at the neighborhood scale. They don't necessarily have to be all the way up here

18:54.706 --> 18:57.920
at your 30-plus thousand people's, you know,

18:57.936 --> 19:01.150
whole community. Sometimes blooming where people are

19:01.650 --> 19:04.862
planted can be a valuable way to foster those community connections.

19:05.167 --> 19:07.670
I'd say that would be number one. Number one that jumps out,

19:07.959 --> 19:11.774
addressing the concerns of residents on things like water,

19:12.062 --> 19:15.717
things like open space. There may be a lot of other things you're doing

19:15.974 --> 19:19.484
already. The survey results can be a point of communication

19:19.677 --> 19:22.818
and conversation. We hear you. We hear you're concerned about

19:23.318 --> 19:26.730
this, and this is what our city's doing. And that can really be kind of

19:27.230 --> 19:30.417
a magic moment where this you can respond to the data.

19:30.898 --> 19:32.789
We provided flyers for you to.

19:39.992 --> 19:43.668
Okay, thank you. We appreciate your stuff, and, uh, we'll be able

19:43.684 --> 19:47.090
to use that information to help make things better. Did you have anything else

19:47.187 --> 19:50.801
to add, Abby? No, I think a lot of it is really

19:51.301 --> 19:53.866
great though to use for planning and things like that, knowing that they want more

19:54.366 --> 19:57.128
retail and more community events. I really like that, so I appreciate them doing the

19:57.628 --> 20:00.888
survey for us. Thank you. Thanks, thanks for the opportunity to drive down.

20:01.145 --> 20:04.773
Yep, appreciate it. The traffic's terrible leaving town, so good

20:05.273 --> 20:08.466
luck. We'll get some snacks. Yeah,

20:08.482 --> 20:12.063
so with that, we also had our community survey,

20:12.079 --> 20:15.676
the annual community survey, and I think we're

20:16.176 --> 20:18.984
gonna run right into that with Evan. Did you have— okay,

20:19.208 --> 20:19.883
yeah, go ahead.

20:24.218 --> 20:27.380
Sorry. Go ahead. Didn't mean to interrupt you. Good afternoon, Mayor and Council.

20:27.878 --> 20:31.570
I'm pleased to introduce Ron Gailey. He is from OnPoint Insights.

20:31.907 --> 20:35.487
We have worked with their company a number of times before on previous surveys,

20:35.583 --> 20:39.098
including our general plan survey, previous annual surveys.

20:39.211 --> 20:42.774
Today he is here to talk with you all about our

20:42.887 --> 20:46.017
annual survey. We do have a basic

