01:00:11.072 --> 01:00:15.352
would be able to run the program for us. And ideally, we would have zero

01:00:15.594 --> 01:00:19.992
requirements from the city. We wouldn't need any hours

01:00:20.491 --> 01:00:24.291
from you guys, except for maybe a few confirmation things and

01:00:24.791 --> 01:00:26.646
things we can't get around.

01:00:28.810 --> 01:00:31.906
The idea— this is a model that we looked at—

01:00:32.406 --> 01:00:36.006
was the Wasatch Community Gardens, which They have assigned plots to

01:00:36.087 --> 01:00:39.192
each to specific residents, and they have an

01:00:39.418 --> 01:00:42.948
each bed has a named steward. They have a remedy period,

01:00:42.964 --> 01:00:46.772
so if someone stops maintaining their plot, they have to fix it within

01:00:46.804 --> 01:00:50.050
a certain amount of time, and then they have a requirement for

01:00:50.500 --> 01:00:52.365
volunteer hours for the gardener.

01:00:55.982 --> 01:01:00.372
We what we had talked about was having a small,

01:01:00.388 --> 01:01:03.721
modest annual plot fee and a minimal monthly maintenance fee that could

01:01:04.221 --> 01:01:07.487
go towards things like, um, what did we say?

01:01:07.968 --> 01:01:11.477
Seeds. Oh, seeds and the, the water and

01:01:11.653 --> 01:01:15.099
fertilizer and things like that. Um, and then we'd have raised

01:01:15.599 --> 01:01:18.976
beds with imported soil, which avoids— I mean, we have rough

01:01:20.178 --> 01:01:22.694
soil here. It's hard, can be hard to grow things. So we would have some

01:01:22.790 --> 01:01:25.883
soil brought in, and then we would just have oversight by

01:01:27.028 --> 01:01:30.696
a subcommittee on the Community Services Board, and then that

01:01:30.728 --> 01:01:34.300
would be— that subcommittee would be mostly

01:01:34.800 --> 01:01:38.071
manned by someone like Jen if that were to be who was voted in.

01:01:40.841 --> 01:01:45.299
Having a drip line makes it so

01:01:45.315 --> 01:01:48.100
that we wouldn't be wasting any water. The water's already there,

01:01:49.016 --> 01:01:52.943
and we wouldn't need to have any— I

01:01:53.443 --> 01:01:56.957
mean, we would still need to have a hose With a little bit, we'd have

01:01:56.989 --> 01:01:59.865
to have water to be able to put in the new plants, but it would

01:01:59.881 --> 01:02:01.077
all be maintained by a drip line.

01:02:04.131 --> 01:02:07.858
And then this was just potential, a range of costs

01:02:07.874 --> 01:02:11.210
of what it might cost to start up. There are things that we have

01:02:13.079 --> 01:02:16.224
added in later phases, like having a fence and a shed,

01:02:17.356 --> 01:02:20.411
that could be done sooner or later, depending on what you guys

01:02:20.444 --> 01:02:25.243
thought about. And then

01:02:25.615 --> 01:02:29.851
the timeline would— we would just need your direction now, and then in

01:02:29.883 --> 01:02:33.000
the fall is when we would like to prepare the site and have it all

01:02:33.500 --> 01:02:34.024
ready to go by spring.

01:02:37.521 --> 01:02:40.318
And so that's what we would need. And do you guys have any questions?

01:02:42.186 --> 01:02:45.506
So that's where the disc golf course is, right? And so

01:02:45.522 --> 01:02:48.974
how And so I guess

01:02:49.474 --> 01:02:52.595
one of the questions you had is, is this going to take what could have

01:02:53.095 --> 01:02:57.445
otherwise been parking? Yes, that is one of the things on there. Some people had

01:02:57.767 --> 01:03:01.039
requested— they had made a request saying that they

01:03:01.055 --> 01:03:04.263
would like parking there, and that would be a good spot for it.

01:03:11.741 --> 01:03:15.550
But there is a water line there today. Yes. In that parking area?

01:03:15.599 --> 01:03:18.876
Mm-hmm. So we've got irrigation running through there, is that

01:03:19.376 --> 01:03:22.825
right? Yeah, as we mentioned earlier,

01:03:23.035 --> 01:03:26.142
I think in a previous meeting when we discussed this, there is a meter

01:03:26.433 --> 01:03:29.703
that's already been set there with a backflow prevention device.

01:03:30.815 --> 01:03:34.367
In the past, we have had community gardens tried

01:03:34.867 --> 01:03:38.549
to be facilitated there twice that I know of, and we did

01:03:39.049 --> 01:03:42.783
the same thing. Where the city basically covered the cost of the seed and

01:03:43.297 --> 01:03:48.742
most of it was native type vegetation

01:03:49.224 --> 01:03:53.416
that would be grown and edible. And so, but we

01:03:53.916 --> 01:03:56.307
just never could get it to take off. And I think the city ended up

01:03:56.807 --> 01:04:00.323
spending about $10,000 through about 2 efforts and we never seen a

01:04:00.403 --> 01:04:03.214
byproduct from it. So that's the history of it.

01:04:03.857 --> 01:04:07.069
But as Jessie reached out, my conversation

01:04:07.569 --> 01:04:10.261
with her was, If we're gonna go back and we're gonna pilot a site,

01:04:10.293 --> 01:04:14.004
I think that we should try the same location and just see if we

01:04:14.504 --> 01:04:17.312
can figure out and get something moving, and then you can figure out how to

01:04:17.812 --> 01:04:21.159
modify from that point. And so that was my recommendation.

01:04:23.877 --> 01:04:27.059
And that way we're not losing as much if it does for some reason fail.

01:04:27.332 --> 01:04:31.092
But as us and the Community Services Board, as we discussed

01:04:31.767 --> 01:04:35.488
with Jen, I think that having her as the

01:04:35.988 --> 01:04:37.853
coordinator for this will make it a win.

01:04:39.449 --> 01:04:42.954
Yes, you can make a comment. Thanks so much.

01:04:43.292 --> 01:04:46.753
Um, I— we've had a lot of people reach out from the community that

01:04:47.253 --> 01:04:50.633
are very, very interested in doing this. We had one guy that came and talked

01:04:51.133 --> 01:04:54.096
for about 45 minutes to us at the farmer's market about it, really interested.

01:04:54.259 --> 01:04:57.554
Older retired people sometimes, and there's so many people who

01:04:57.668 --> 01:05:00.869
want to know how to garden. but don't know how.

01:05:01.704 --> 01:05:05.219
And especially with our economy, I think that it is an important

01:05:05.315 --> 01:05:09.391
thing that we try and feed our community. So even

01:05:09.504 --> 01:05:13.677
though I would be over it, I would recruit

01:05:13.741 --> 01:05:17.111
other people. And I think that is going to make the difference of whether

01:05:17.192 --> 01:05:20.722
it succeeds or fails, is if we have people who are

01:05:21.222 --> 01:05:24.189
really interested in doing it, who have the time, who want to donate that,

01:05:24.655 --> 01:05:28.057
and actually make it happen. And I think it also needs

01:05:28.105 --> 01:05:32.182
to be food plants, things that they will actually

01:05:32.359 --> 01:05:35.617
eat, rather than just something that is native that's

01:05:35.745 --> 01:05:39.597
edible. I think if we're, you know, and if we do it

01:05:40.097 --> 01:05:43.048
right, the right way, I think that it could be really successful. And it could—

01:05:43.353 --> 01:05:47.767
we could possibly have several of these throughout the community. So the

01:05:48.267 --> 01:05:51.657
people that are being drawn in into the Can they be from

01:05:51.721 --> 01:05:55.580
anywhere in the city, or is this going to be like a zone restricted

01:05:56.080 --> 01:05:58.971
only within a certain area? I think— Do you just go out and sign up

01:05:58.987 --> 01:06:02.547
for it and go farm? I think anybody in the city. I don't

01:06:03.047 --> 01:06:06.416
think it necessarily has to be from that neighborhood. And then if it turns

01:06:06.916 --> 01:06:08.909
out good, then we— okay, we put it into another neighborhood.

01:06:11.860 --> 01:06:15.267
So part of what— just to clarify what Brad was talking about too

01:06:15.767 --> 01:06:19.638
with the physical Financial impact and things like that. My understanding is at this

01:06:20.138 --> 01:06:23.989
point, very little to zero staff time and no financial

