with city staff. They did not exist when we put in the original,

02:10:21.220 --> 02:10:24.826
uh, Gateway Park improvements with the storage garages, the flex space,

02:10:24.890 --> 02:10:28.223
the Enbridge facility, etc. So there was no contemplation

02:10:28.723 --> 02:10:33.012
to try to work an internal road to try to create like a little horseshoe

02:10:33.028 --> 02:10:36.192
or half moon is just a waste of property, I think, on a

02:10:36.692 --> 02:10:40.444
6-acre lot. The other lots are not 6 acres, but they're also significant in size.

02:10:42.179 --> 02:10:45.773
And the south side of Wagstaff Way, so between

02:10:45.918 --> 02:10:49.580
Wagstaff Way and State Road 73, is a tapering

02:10:49.596 --> 02:10:52.939
traffic route according to our modeling because it connects to

02:10:53.439 --> 02:10:57.380
a right-in, right-out. Most of the traffic will, in the modeling,

02:10:57.412 --> 02:11:01.427
will either go to Spring Run Parkway, the signalized

02:11:01.491 --> 02:11:04.798
full-service intersection when UDOT expands that facility,

02:11:05.296 --> 02:11:08.989
or it'll go eastbound to Mount Airy Drive where there's another signalized

02:11:09.053 --> 02:11:12.650
intersection. Scott, that was one of the questions that I had. Mayor, may I just

02:11:13.150 --> 02:11:16.728
jump in here? And it's really, so until UDOT does the expansion,

02:11:17.066 --> 02:11:20.998
what is the plan for the connectivity to Whatever

02:11:21.498 --> 02:11:24.303
the name of this road is going to be. Yeah, this kind of the S-shape

02:11:24.803 --> 02:11:27.431
that you have right now for the right-in, right-out, is that going to— are you

02:11:27.931 --> 02:11:31.425
guys— is that going to be developed? So that's what we'll connect to. That S-curve

02:11:31.925 --> 02:11:35.115
has been paved from— I mean, it's been paved for a really long time.

02:11:35.676 --> 02:11:39.671
So that was permitted when we sold the corridor preservation

02:11:39.703 --> 02:11:42.414
parcel to UDOT. We sold them about 8 acres along our frontage.

02:11:43.713 --> 02:11:46.793
We wanted to maintain the access. We put the permit in.

02:11:47.162 --> 02:11:50.855
Typically, the permit requires a road like that to be on a property

02:11:51.355 --> 02:11:54.324
line, but in this case it didn't because of the topography.

02:11:54.532 --> 02:11:57.872
We had to create— UDOT required us to create a safe egress

02:11:58.081 --> 02:12:01.597
road, which required us to kind of run that up parallel grade and

02:12:02.097 --> 02:12:05.676
snake it up the hill. So that's why it's snaky, is because it's going

02:12:05.933 --> 02:12:08.919
up that hill. The property line's smack in the middle of it,

02:12:08.935 --> 02:12:12.098
if you can see that yellow line. So when UDOT's facilities in there, what does

02:12:12.114 --> 02:12:14.924
that look like? Is it a right-in, right-out right at the base of where the

02:12:15.424 --> 02:12:19.517
mayor's cursor's at? It'll tie to a frontage road. So when UDOT expands— so

02:12:20.017 --> 02:12:23.129
for now it goes to the right-in, right-out on the existing facility. Right now the

02:12:23.629 --> 02:12:27.304
program— the programming for the expansion design is when it's completed, it'll tie

02:12:27.804 --> 02:12:31.639
to the frontage road, and then the freeway or arterial traffic

02:12:32.139 --> 02:12:35.573
will go in the middle, similar to what you see at 2100 North. I think

02:12:36.073 --> 02:12:39.218
it's a similar road profile. Thank you. I think what I was gonna say,

02:12:39.973 --> 02:12:43.328
my point when I was talking about the C or the half-moon entrances

02:12:43.425 --> 02:12:46.845
onto the lot, was there residential lots and they're backing out?

02:12:47.198 --> 02:12:50.698
There won't be anybody backing out onto the collector. No, all of this

02:12:50.763 --> 02:12:54.889
is charter school, and then there's these other 2 lots here that are intended to

02:12:54.905 --> 02:12:58.133
mirror a use that's similar to what we did here, some flex space or something

02:12:58.633 --> 02:13:02.227
along those lines. So showing these entrances, they're gonna be pulling out into the road

02:13:02.727 --> 02:13:06.159
just like any other? Correct. And you'll see that on the site plan for

02:13:06.659 --> 02:13:09.512
the charter school when it comes before you to determine whether or not the way

02:13:10.012 --> 02:13:12.255
they're proposing that access and circulation makes sense to you.

02:13:13.459 --> 02:13:17.133
But from what I've seen, they're looking to access one here on Wagstaff Way and

02:13:17.149 --> 02:13:20.293
then circulate out here onto Gateway Park Boulevard.

02:13:20.839 --> 02:13:24.513
If I could just say one thing about Gateway Park Boulevard, obviously we're not

02:13:25.013 --> 02:13:28.235
excited to brand a major master planned community in another city,

02:13:28.379 --> 02:13:33.078
which is why we're proposing the name change. And because we're a significant grouping

02:13:33.110 --> 02:13:36.879
or massing of land, we've got about 600 acres there eventually, we wanted

02:13:37.379 --> 02:13:40.680
it to be destination-oriented. We're— when

02:13:40.712 --> 02:13:43.550
you're on Ranches Parkway, you're in the ranches and you know that.

02:13:43.631 --> 02:13:48.153
So, but Wild Blossom is primarily an east-west road.

02:13:48.730 --> 02:13:52.162
It only curves to the south at the very

02:13:52.339 --> 02:13:56.125
last little bit to connect into our north property line. So the

02:13:56.625 --> 02:14:00.334
vast majority of that right-of-way going north-south would be a whole

02:14:00.382 --> 02:14:04.559
different coordinate set. So I think that should be taken into consideration

02:14:05.059 --> 02:14:08.720
in addition to we're crossing a boundary with another city. And I just wanted to

02:14:08.736 --> 02:14:12.061
add one more thing as you talk master plan for the council to consider.

02:14:12.576 --> 02:14:16.046
If the entrances are, say, here and here that we just talked about,

02:14:16.833 --> 02:14:21.492
without a master plan, is there any kind of an assurance

02:14:21.992 --> 02:14:24.992
that we could have that The future development of something over here is not going

02:14:25.492 --> 02:14:28.460
to have an entrance, you know, they're going to line up is what I'm getting

02:14:28.556 --> 02:14:33.036
at. They're not going to be offset intersections so that if you have an entrance,

02:14:33.148 --> 02:14:36.648
you know, here, that you'd also have one here to a business on

02:14:36.745 --> 02:14:40.309
this side. Yeah, so this,

02:14:40.534 --> 02:14:43.921
this is programmed as a commercial lot. Um,

02:14:45.350 --> 02:14:49.748
so, Mayor, I get the, I get the point, and I think we can work

02:14:50.248 --> 02:14:53.438
to make sure that that's safe. I'm sure whatever we bring for development on this,

02:14:53.454 --> 02:14:56.984
we would collaborate with staff and try to have some intelligent safe design there.

02:14:57.786 --> 02:15:01.941
This parcel is becoming more and more constrained due to the RCA

02:15:02.441 --> 02:15:05.728
approval that at best is creating an 18-foot

02:15:06.228 --> 02:15:09.626
cut where it hits my southern property line, which really affects the ability—

02:15:10.461 --> 02:15:13.749
like, I can't load from this lot on that whole side, and I can't

02:15:14.249 --> 02:15:18.177
for hundreds and hundreds of feet to the north because I just— it's a tunnel.

02:15:18.899 --> 02:15:22.813
So I'm going to have to load somewhat from Wagstaff Way

02:15:23.313 --> 02:15:26.390
or from this side to be able to even make that property work. I just

02:15:26.890 --> 02:15:30.754
want to make sure there's some kind of contemplation of lining that up.

02:15:32.181 --> 02:15:35.823
This— what if you're going to load off this side, that there's a—

02:15:35.951 --> 02:15:39.175
they're across from each other? Yeah, I think we could work to make sure that

02:15:39.675 --> 02:15:43.210
that makes sense moving forward. Just know that it'll most likely have to load from

02:15:43.710 --> 02:15:46.953
that side because we have new constraints that we didn't anticipate on the

02:15:56.164 --> 02:15:59.689
I'm ready to make a motion. I think we should call it IU Boulevard instead,

02:16:00.126 --> 02:16:03.247
but I'll make this motion. Craig,

02:16:03.344 --> 02:16:05.252
before you do that, can I just ask a question? Oh, yeah, go ahead.

02:16:05.449 --> 02:16:09.032
How does the council feel about the name? I think Gateway Park Boulevard.

02:16:09.113 --> 02:16:11.307
I like it. It comes to me, it comes down to branding, and we're a

02:16:11.807 --> 02:16:15.180
different city, so I'm okay having it be— Okay, that's what I wanted to hear.

02:16:16.380 --> 02:16:19.827
Okay, so I move that City Council approve the Gateway Park Plat 3 preliminary

02:16:19.956 --> 02:16:23.225
plat with condition number 1,

02:16:23.805 --> 02:16:26.920
omitting condition number 2. Second.

02:16:31.035 --> 02:16:34.503
Okay, we have a motion by Councilmember Whiting and a second by Councilmember Clark,

02:16:34.519 --> 02:16:38.171
and we'll start the vote with Councilmember Clark? Yes. Councilmember Wright?

02:16:38.348 --> 02:16:40.965
Yes. Councilmember Whiting? Yes. Councilmember Hewish?

02:16:41.607 --> 02:16:43.887
Yes. Okay, that motion passes.

02:16:45.316 --> 02:16:49.619
Thank you. Appreciate your time. And we'll move to item 14—

02:16:50.117 --> 02:16:53.521
nope, 15A. And that's an ordinance of Eagle Mountain City,

02:16:53.569 --> 02:16:57.583
Utah, amending the Eagle Mountain Municipal Code to repeal

02:16:58.083 --> 02:17:00.827
and replace Section 1760130,

02:17:01.020 --> 02:17:04.074
regarding retaining walls and construction standards.

02:17:05.776 --> 02:17:08.820
Um, we'll invite Vince. There you are.

02:17:09.536 --> 02:17:12.201
You probably see me here a little better, I think.

02:17:21.235 --> 02:17:24.245
Mayor and Council, it's good to be with you. We'll get this pulled up here

02:17:24.745 --> 02:17:25.204
and we can get started.

02:17:47.119 --> 02:17:50.699
Okay. I'll warn you, this is a

02:17:51.199 --> 02:17:54.422
lot of engineering and technical stuff that we're going to talk about.

02:17:54.900 --> 02:17:56.886
Hopefully you have your engineering hats on.

02:18:00.273 --> 02:18:03.622
As I've been here for the last couple years, this is

02:18:03.718 --> 02:18:06.262
one of the codes that has come up kind of over and over again,

02:18:07.093 --> 02:18:10.861
not just from an engineering standpoint, but from planning and from our building

02:18:10.893 --> 02:18:14.919
inspectors. And it became pretty obvious

02:18:15.419 --> 02:18:18.403
that there were some weaknesses in the previous code that we wanted to address.

02:18:19.113 --> 02:18:23.253
And so, This is a conglomeration

02:18:23.447 --> 02:18:26.723
of engineering and planning and the building

02:18:26.755 --> 02:18:30.247
inspectors all meeting together and kind of coming up with what we think is best.

02:18:36.512 --> 02:18:38.184
So why we're updating the standard.

02:18:39.884 --> 02:18:43.390
The old standard, if you remember, it had

02:18:43.920 --> 02:18:47.105
specific types of materials called out

02:18:47.605 --> 02:18:51.244
and And it was kind of based on those specific types of walls

02:18:51.744 --> 02:18:55.050
or types of materials of walls. We wanted to go away from

02:18:55.066 --> 02:18:57.989
that a little bit and base it more on an engineering basis.

02:18:59.855 --> 02:19:03.043
There was nothing in the old code about surcharge loading on

02:19:03.092 --> 02:19:06.924
top of these walls. And we'll talk a little bit more about what surcharge

02:19:07.020 --> 02:19:10.150
load is in just a minute. But we wanted to address that.

02:19:11.699 --> 02:19:15.015
Height limits, a set 6-foot for absolutely

02:19:15.515 --> 02:19:18.713
everything. A lot of requests for variances to this,

02:19:19.228 --> 02:19:21.178
and so we tried to address that one too.

02:19:22.582 --> 02:19:25.728
And then in the old code, there was no formal inspection program

02:19:26.228 --> 02:19:30.020
required. And so once it got through the city review process,

02:19:30.084 --> 02:19:33.829
they would go out and build it, and it wasn't always built according to what

02:19:34.329 --> 02:19:38.225
we had specced out. So to

02:19:38.503 --> 02:19:41.798
kind of simplify things, we said, let's just divide

02:19:42.298 --> 02:19:46.035
this into 2 main categories, prescriptive gravity

02:19:46.535 --> 02:19:49.984
walls and engineering retaining walls. The gravity

02:19:50.000 --> 02:19:52.657
walls are just 4-foot walls maximum.

02:19:53.253 --> 02:19:56.811
Someone wants to go in their backyard and build a kind of a rock wall

02:19:56.860 --> 02:20:00.386
in their backyard, as long as it's 4-foot tall and no surcharge

02:20:00.886 --> 02:20:04.445
on the top, that's perfectly fine. No permit required.

02:20:04.525 --> 02:20:08.240
The city would still look at it, obviously, but no building permit required for

02:20:08.257 --> 02:20:12.056
that. And the

02:20:12.072 --> 02:20:15.709
one thing we would look at is surcharging. You know, are we parking cars on

02:20:16.209 --> 02:20:18.938
top of this? Is it being built up for a parking pad or an RV

02:20:19.438 --> 02:20:23.059
pad? Is a building or a shed or something

02:20:23.107 --> 02:20:26.021
going to be on top of it? Those are the kind of things that you

02:20:26.521 --> 02:20:30.140
would see in a surcharge situation. And then the other

02:20:30.890 --> 02:20:34.598
thing you'd look at for surcharges is the Is there

02:20:34.646 --> 02:20:37.689
a slope on top of the wall that comes up to the top of the

02:20:38.189 --> 02:20:40.749
wall, and is it flat or is it actually sloped? Because if it's sloped,

02:20:40.765 --> 02:20:43.504
then there's actually more loading on that because of the slope.

02:20:45.026 --> 02:20:47.812
So those are the things we looked at, and this is kind of the way

02:20:47.860 --> 02:20:49.059
we defined our two walls.

02:20:53.053 --> 02:20:56.480
So this was the main complaint from the the building inspectors: is

02:20:56.496 --> 02:20:59.913
it wasn't clear when actual building. Permit was

02:20:59.929 --> 02:21:03.767
needed and when it was not. And so we tried to clarify this

02:21:04.267 --> 02:21:06.994
in saying, you know, the gravity walls, 4-foot, no surcharge,

02:21:07.604 --> 02:21:10.864
then no building permit. If it's above 4-foot

02:21:11.538 --> 02:21:14.445
or if it has any kind of surcharge, no matter what the height,

02:21:14.846 --> 02:21:18.603
it would definitely need a building permit to go

02:21:19.103 --> 02:21:23.035
forward with that. So we tried to fix that little issue

02:21:27.371 --> 02:21:31.328
Height standards. Like I mentioned before, 6 foot was,

02:21:31.928 --> 02:21:34.862
was really the max for any type of wall height.

02:21:36.852 --> 02:21:40.946
What we have proposed in this is that in residential properties, keep that same

02:21:41.237 --> 02:21:44.360
6 foot height. I think that's reasonable for most residential properties.

02:21:45.120 --> 02:21:48.871
And then in the commercial industrial areas where they have bigger

02:21:48.920 --> 02:21:51.812
buildings and bigger parking lots that need more flat area,

02:21:53.407 --> 02:21:56.692
move that up to 10 foot to allow them to flatten things

02:21:56.773 --> 02:21:58.367
out and to use their property.

02:22:00.372 --> 02:22:03.922
And then obviously that 10 foot is clearly in the building permit

02:22:04.422 --> 02:22:07.957
range, and it's also in the range where we would look at

02:22:08.457 --> 02:22:09.554
it really close on an engineering standpoint.

02:22:16.620 --> 02:22:19.383
Multiple tiers. of retaining walls.

02:22:19.640 --> 02:22:24.164
This was in the previous code, but the tier length

02:22:24.212 --> 02:22:27.036
between the walls was 1.5 times the height.

02:22:28.079 --> 02:22:32.732
We propose to change that, and this came from the building inspector's request

02:22:33.232 --> 02:22:36.519
that that is changed to 2 to 1. So if

02:22:37.019 --> 02:22:39.920
you have a 6-foot wall, it's 12 foot before you can actually put another retaining

02:22:39.936 --> 02:22:43.723
wall. And that actually corresponds with the

02:22:43.739 --> 02:22:47.355
International Building Code, and so we're actually just kind of mirroring What

02:22:47.855 --> 02:22:50.304
the International Building Code, or IBC, says on that one.

02:22:52.376 --> 02:22:56.168
And then, if for some reason it has to be less than

02:22:56.184 --> 02:22:59.507
that, if there's constraints in the property, then it has to be built

02:23:00.007 --> 02:23:03.001
and designed as a single wall system.

02:23:04.815 --> 02:23:08.138
And in engineering terms, it basically means they have to do

02:23:08.638 --> 02:23:11.867
the geotech for both walls, looking at them both not independently as different

02:23:12.367 --> 02:23:13.398
walls, but as one wall system.

02:23:15.843 --> 02:23:19.419
So, as part of that, and actually this applies not

02:23:19.467 --> 02:23:22.897
just to the tiers, but to everything, we better defined exactly

02:23:22.914 --> 02:23:26.452
what was submitted to the engineering department and to planning

02:23:27.051 --> 02:23:29.240
as far as engineering requirements.

02:23:31.961 --> 02:23:35.606
In the old code, it basically had to be stamped by a professional

02:23:36.106 --> 02:23:38.425
engineer, and we kind of relied on that stamp.

02:23:39.451 --> 02:23:42.791
Now we're actually going to be reviewing a lot

02:23:43.291 --> 02:23:46.307
more information— lateral pressure, seismic loading,

02:23:46.644 --> 02:23:50.530
the drainage, bearing capacity, those kind of things— so that we're looking

02:23:51.030 --> 02:23:54.014
specifically at each wall, because each wall is, is going to be a little bit

02:23:54.062 --> 02:23:57.739
different. And so we want to look at the calculations behind

02:23:57.980 --> 02:24:01.224
where they got to and just make sure that we agree with those things before

02:24:01.336 --> 02:24:03.937
we're approving those on an engineering basis. Thank you.

02:24:07.699 --> 02:24:10.910
And then I mentioned this one, inspection requirements.

02:24:11.394 --> 02:24:15.477
The previous code didn't have any inspection requirements, and this defines—

02:24:16.332 --> 02:24:18.684
there's a list of multiple inspections in there.

02:24:20.584 --> 02:24:23.708
We don't necessarily have to do every single inspection that's listed

02:24:24.208 --> 02:24:27.919
in there, but it says we can, and it really allows our building inspectors to

02:24:28.419 --> 02:24:31.116
be able to go out and check they need to check and, and look at

02:24:31.616 --> 02:24:34.113
the things that they want to look at as these things are getting built.

02:24:35.700 --> 02:24:38.786
Um, the one exception to that

02:24:39.286 --> 02:24:42.567
is the mechanically stabilized earth walls.

02:24:43.702 --> 02:24:46.794
Those walls, um, they're the walls that you

02:24:47.294 --> 02:24:51.100
see UDOT put up when they're building a roadway way, way up

02:24:51.600 --> 02:24:57.200
above, and, and they're 20, 30-foot tall walls, and it's— it looks like concrete panel

02:24:57.700 --> 02:24:59.421
paneling is what it looks like. But those panels are anchored back into the soil,

02:24:59.534 --> 02:25:03.037
and so the soil behind it is really what, what is holding that wall

02:25:03.085 --> 02:25:06.588
up. And that's— it's very critical to get proper materials,

02:25:06.861 --> 02:25:10.766
proper compaction, and those kind of things. And so that's why we said on those

02:25:10.798 --> 02:25:14.269
walls, we, we want to— we will require

02:25:14.896 --> 02:25:19.636
inspections on those, and we want to see the tests on the compaction

02:25:20.136 --> 02:25:22.800
and those kind of things, just so we're We're making sure those are being built

02:25:23.943 --> 02:25:24.597
in a safe manner.

02:25:28.878 --> 02:25:30.825
And then property line and easement standards.

02:25:32.723 --> 02:25:36.510
The old standard was the wall, the face of the wall had to be 3

02:25:37.010 --> 02:25:40.134
feet from a property line. And we're actually saying, yeah,

02:25:40.150 --> 02:25:43.371
we kind of agree with that because it clearly defines on whose

02:25:43.404 --> 02:25:47.061
property that wall is. And if there's an issue with it, it's clearly

02:25:47.094 --> 02:25:50.899
defined who needs to deal with it or address it. Where if it's right on

02:25:50.931 --> 02:25:54.640
the property line, I think that's where this code originated from. If it's right

02:25:55.140 --> 02:25:57.434
on the property line and there's an issue with it, then you have the 2

02:25:57.934 --> 02:26:01.352
neighbors arguing over whose wall it is and who built it and

02:26:02.090 --> 02:26:05.494
those kind of things. And so I think to avoid that, that's where this code

02:26:05.994 --> 02:26:09.653
originated from. And we propose that

02:26:10.135 --> 02:26:13.409
we keep that language in here. Now, we did add some

02:26:13.909 --> 02:26:17.099
language. If you have a retaining wall, like on the back of a property that's

02:26:17.599 --> 02:26:20.966
going on multiple lots, there's no reason to

02:26:21.466 --> 02:26:24.864
do a small retaining wall and then stop, and then have another retaining wall in

02:26:24.961 --> 02:26:28.426
the next lot and stop, and have another. We can have a continuous

02:26:28.926 --> 02:26:32.292
retaining wall across all of those. But in that case,

02:26:33.576 --> 02:26:36.881
it's typically built by the developer, and he's building

02:26:36.929 --> 02:26:39.975
it as one cohesive. coherent or one cohesive system.

02:26:41.433 --> 02:26:45.610
And so we would be looking at that as the developer comes in and

02:26:45.643 --> 02:26:49.464
comes in for approval and those kind of things. But we said that that

02:26:49.529 --> 02:26:52.976
is allowable to go across property lines

02:26:53.073 --> 02:26:54.448
in a perpendicular manner.

02:26:59.226 --> 02:27:03.335
Vince? Yes. With that, it talks about how if all owners

02:27:03.384 --> 02:27:07.128
agree At that point when it's being approved and

02:27:07.628 --> 02:27:11.188
whatnot by the city to a developer, really probably only

02:27:11.688 --> 02:27:15.662
has one owner at that point. Are we looking to get some

02:27:15.936 --> 02:27:19.374
future owner information, or is there going to be some sort of

02:27:19.406 --> 02:27:23.361
disclosure in like the real estate documents?

02:27:23.426 --> 02:27:26.757
Like, or is it just better

02:27:27.257 --> 02:27:30.753
than it used to be? Right, right. We didn't

02:27:31.253 --> 02:27:34.665
define that in this because we were mostly just looking at the technical side.

02:27:34.730 --> 02:27:38.254
But yeah, I think what we were thinking is if you come

02:27:38.302 --> 02:27:42.387
in after the fact, 2 neighbors want to put in one retaining wall and

02:27:42.565 --> 02:27:45.597
they're separate owners. If it comes in as a developer,

02:27:46.214 --> 02:27:49.359
I think it's the same owner and they can still put it in how they

02:27:49.859 --> 02:27:50.607
want. Okay, thank you.

02:27:53.003 --> 02:27:57.680
Yep. The other change that we had was the The PUE that

02:27:58.049 --> 02:28:01.631
is on the back side of most lots, or even on the side

02:28:01.888 --> 02:28:05.679
of most of our lots in the city, that public utility

02:28:06.179 --> 02:28:10.145
easement has some restrictions. And in the old code, it said

02:28:10.418 --> 02:28:14.016
we couldn't have any kind of retaining wall on the PUE, which was

02:28:14.516 --> 02:28:17.967
actually a conflict with the 3-foot rule because most of our PUEs are either 5-foot

02:28:17.983 --> 02:28:22.532
or 10-foot offset from that property line. So with

02:28:23.032 --> 02:28:26.786
that one, we basically modified it to be similar to if someone

02:28:27.543 --> 02:28:30.397
comes in and wants to put a sign in our PUE,

02:28:31.496 --> 02:28:34.664
they have to come to us for an exception. It has to be looked at

02:28:34.728 --> 02:28:38.204
and approved, but we would allow retaining walls in that PUE now with

02:28:38.704 --> 02:28:38.737
the new code.

02:28:43.408 --> 02:28:47.090
So yeah, that's really a summary of all the changes we made to

02:28:47.590 --> 02:28:50.750
the code. Open it up for any questions you guys

02:28:50.783 --> 02:28:50.993
have.

02:28:58.080 --> 02:29:01.292
I don't really have a question, maybe just a comment, Vince. I mean,

02:29:02.148 --> 02:29:05.700
great job, number one. Thank you for taking an engineer's look

02:29:06.200 --> 02:29:09.166
at this. I think it's actually really well done.

02:29:09.754 --> 02:29:14.123
My only concern is with 5B, and I just I,

02:29:14.204 --> 02:29:18.280
I, I don't think we should leave multifamily properties in the, in the approved

02:29:18.780 --> 02:29:22.357
for a 10-foot wall height because I think that just encourages hillside development,

02:29:22.373 --> 02:29:25.744
which I want to stay away from anyway. So that would be my only comment,

02:29:25.824 --> 02:29:28.937
that, and that's something I'd ask the council to do is consider removing the word

02:29:28.954 --> 02:29:32.886
multifamily from 5B. Obviously we have

02:29:33.386 --> 02:29:35.630
a public hearing and, and, and other things, so I don't really have any other

02:29:36.130 --> 02:29:39.963
questions. That, that's my only comment. Just my, my question being, So the 3-foot

02:29:40.027 --> 02:29:42.913
wall face, does it really have to be 3 feet to make sure people know

02:29:43.413 --> 02:29:46.712
whose wall it is? Can we make it a little sooner, closer? That's what he

02:29:47.212 --> 02:29:50.543
changed. No, I think he said that's what it was. Yeah, it's still 3

02:29:51.043 --> 02:29:54.631
feet from the property line. Yeah, but the footing is 1 foot. It's 1 foot

02:29:55.131 --> 02:29:57.886
for the footing. So you're 1 foot from the footing from the property line,

02:29:57.918 --> 02:30:00.771
and then you're the center of the wall. So if you have a 2-foot,

02:30:01.781 --> 02:30:04.843
I guess it's a little further away than that. It just depends how big the

02:30:05.343 --> 02:30:09.023
footing is. Right, right. And it doesn't necessarily have to be

02:30:09.523 --> 02:30:13.044
three foot, but it would have a footing on the wall. So if you're

02:30:13.108 --> 02:30:16.473
on the lower side of that, even though the face of

02:30:16.973 --> 02:30:19.590
the wall is three foot, or maybe it would be close, take care of it.

02:30:19.671 --> 02:30:22.885
If if it's built right on the property line, now you're in your neighbor's yard

02:30:23.385 --> 02:30:25.863
taking care of it if you need to. So for me, that makes complete sense.

02:30:28.623 --> 02:30:31.919
The other question I had was. Maybe I missed

02:30:32.419 --> 02:30:35.885
it, but I think it's 6B. When you're talking— or

02:30:35.949 --> 02:30:39.447
is it C? 6A or C?

02:30:39.963 --> 02:30:43.104
When you're talking about tiered walls, so the base wall and then the second

02:30:43.604 --> 02:30:48.095
wall that's tiered back. Yep. There's no definition, at least I couldn't—

02:30:48.595 --> 02:30:51.487
you have the length, but is there that gap that you're asking for? It doesn't

02:30:51.987 --> 02:30:55.359
say how much. Twice the width. 12 feet? I mean,

02:30:55.375 --> 02:30:56.954
you can have— if you have So if you go 4 feet and you go

02:30:57.454 --> 02:31:00.888
back 2 feet and go another 4? Right, then it's considered a

02:31:01.388 --> 02:31:04.918
single wall. Yep. So yeah.

02:31:05.448 --> 02:31:08.659
So then my question is, even on the industrial

02:31:09.159 --> 02:31:12.754
at 10 feet, I used to build a lot of these, and whether it's

02:31:12.770 --> 02:31:16.527
block or whatever, at some point on anything over 6 feet,

02:31:16.543 --> 02:31:20.364
they'd have to have a mesh or an engineered

02:31:21.987 --> 02:31:25.485
Yeah, kind of an anchor system. Anchor. Yeah, I'm sorry. That would also

02:31:25.534 --> 02:31:29.000
get buried behind that wall to keep the middle of the wall from bulging out.

02:31:29.097 --> 02:31:33.369
Sure. So is that also— if

02:31:33.869 --> 02:31:36.623
we're going to go to 10 feet, I'm just curious if that's something that we

02:31:37.123 --> 02:31:40.669
need to, especially if it's load-bearing. Yeah,

02:31:41.215 --> 02:31:44.334
that is something we would look at in our engineering review. We didn't spell that

02:31:44.834 --> 02:31:48.157
out specifically in the code, but in the It requires a permit.

02:31:48.734 --> 02:31:52.131
Yeah, it requires a permit, and it requires submittals of all

02:31:52.179 --> 02:31:55.288
the calculations. And so as part of those calculation checks, we would

02:31:55.788 --> 02:31:58.364
look at that. Does it need to be anchored to have stability? Do you think

02:31:58.749 --> 02:32:01.890
that the code would protect us a little bit knowing that anything over 6 feet

02:32:02.390 --> 02:32:05.656
is going to most likely require that, that we should

02:32:06.156 --> 02:32:09.902
at least say something like that? Right. I think

02:32:09.950 --> 02:32:13.621
with our engineering check, we could check and verify those

02:32:13.653 --> 02:32:17.421
things as they come in. We could add it to the code explicitly if

02:32:17.921 --> 02:32:21.768
you want, but I think it's— Is there any— is there

02:32:22.268 --> 02:32:24.286
ever a time when you're going over 6 feet and we're talking about 10 feet

02:32:24.786 --> 02:32:28.586
that you wouldn't require it? Yes, it would depend on the type of

02:32:29.086 --> 02:32:32.643
the wall that they're building. Like a thick concrete wall with a big footing on

02:32:32.692 --> 02:32:34.838
it wouldn't necessarily have to have anchors on it.

02:32:36.268 --> 02:32:38.821
So yeah, it's a solid wall. If it's a solid wall.

02:32:39.740 --> 02:32:41.222
Correct, correct. Solid wall.

02:32:44.030 --> 02:32:47.527
Yep. If you feel comfortable. Okay, so is there any other questions

02:32:48.027 --> 02:32:50.620
for Vince? We do have a public hearing, so nothing there.

02:32:51.302 --> 02:32:53.540
All right, thank you. Um,

02:32:55.408 --> 02:32:58.757
with that, it is 7:58 and this is

02:32:58.805 --> 02:33:02.645
a public hearing, so I will open the public hearing and ask Lacey

02:33:02.677 --> 02:33:05.874
if anyone— nobody turned in anything. Is there anyone

02:33:05.939 --> 02:33:08.941
here that would like to speak on this item that did not get the opportunity?

02:33:09.969 --> 02:33:13.309
Seeing no one, at 7:58, we will close the public hearing

02:33:13.405 --> 02:33:16.664
and bring it back to the council for further discussion. I'll make a motion unless

02:33:16.777 --> 02:33:20.711
there's concerns. Anybody have anything they want to add before

02:33:21.211 --> 02:33:24.966
we do that? Melissa? I just— I want to say this. I'm really grateful

02:33:25.466 --> 02:33:28.835
that we're doing this. I think that engineering and staff did a really great

02:33:29.335 --> 02:33:33.552
job in preparing this code. But some of them are contingent

02:33:33.632 --> 02:33:36.920
on whoever is working in the department at the

02:33:37.420 --> 02:33:40.672
time and what inspections

02:33:40.928 --> 02:33:44.424
and things that they're going to require. I want us to always make sure

02:33:44.456 --> 02:33:48.481
that the culture of our city is that we require the

02:33:48.981 --> 02:33:52.779
inspections and the engineering plans and all of those things because there are places in

02:33:52.811 --> 02:33:55.825
our city where this hasn't been done, and I want

02:33:56.325 --> 02:33:59.807
to make sure that that doesn't change. as we have different staff members over

02:34:00.307 --> 02:34:04.210
the years. So I think I'm calling on our Building and Engineering

02:34:04.274 --> 02:34:08.078
and Community Development Department to just really make sure that we

02:34:08.578 --> 02:34:12.710
make these inspections and permits

02:34:13.210 --> 02:34:15.910
our goal and our culture in our city. Thank you.

02:34:18.979 --> 02:34:22.210
Entertain a motion, Mayor. I move that the Eagle Mountain

02:34:22.710 --> 02:34:25.847
City Council approve an ordinance amending the municipal code

02:34:26.347 --> 02:34:29.979
to repeal and replace Section 17.60.130 regarding

02:34:30.043 --> 02:34:33.899
retaining wall construction standards with the change

02:34:33.980 --> 02:34:37.552
of removing the multifamily, the word multifamily

02:34:37.601 --> 02:34:40.079
from item 5B. Second.

02:34:41.740 --> 02:34:44.541
Okay, we have a motion and a second. The motion by Councilmember Wright and a

02:34:45.041 --> 02:34:48.484
second by Councilmember Whiting. Do we— sorry, go ahead.

02:34:48.790 --> 02:34:52.681
Do we need to have Can we have them move or in front

02:34:53.181 --> 02:34:57.418
of institutional, or is that inherent for staff

02:34:57.918 --> 02:35:00.902
to be able to make that change? I'll amend my motion to include the word

02:35:00.950 --> 02:35:04.563
or. Thank you. Second. Okay,

02:35:05.045 --> 02:35:07.405
is there any other questions on the motion? The, uh,

02:35:08.818 --> 02:35:12.190
motion stands with Councilmember Wright and a second by Councilmember Whiting, and we'll start the

02:35:12.690 --> 02:35:16.156
vote with Councilmember Wright. Yes. Councilmember Whiting. Yes. Councilmember Huish? Yes.

02:35:16.204 --> 02:35:18.918
And Councilmember Clark? Yes. Okay,

02:35:19.255 --> 02:35:22.579
that item passes, and we will move to item 5B.

02:35:23.430 --> 02:35:26.127
This is a— or sorry,

02:35:26.159 --> 02:35:29.772
15B, you're right. An ordinance of Eagle Mountain

02:35:30.272 --> 02:35:34.091
City rezoning certain lands known as the Pinnacles, approving the Pinnacles Master Development

02:35:34.591 --> 02:35:37.142
Plan and Master Development Agreement Amendment.

02:35:37.865 --> 02:36:00.600
And I believe Robert is ready. Good evening,

02:36:01.100 --> 02:36:04.800
Mayor and Council. At the heart of this particular matter is simply a

02:36:05.300 --> 02:36:08.475
request to rezone 3 parcels, properties of

02:36:08.491 --> 02:36:12.425
land, aggregated about 14 acres— sorry for the typo in

02:36:12.925 --> 02:36:16.423
the report— from their commercial neighborhood setting or zoning

02:36:16.520 --> 02:36:20.710
to agriculture. This is based on a request, of course, by an applicant

02:36:21.513 --> 02:36:24.821
representing that they have an interest in temporarily making this happen

02:36:24.885 --> 02:36:28.658
to effectively save money on their tax bill, understandably so.

02:36:29.927 --> 02:36:35.611
The zoning assigned to the project which

02:36:35.643 --> 02:36:39.111
you see on the screen before you as far as location.

02:36:39.159 --> 02:36:42.531
That's the Pinnacles, which is over north of Aviator and west

02:36:43.031 --> 02:36:47.059
of Pony Express. Was something that was inculcated into a development

02:36:47.559 --> 02:36:51.041
agreement memorialized back in the fall of 2022. At that

02:36:51.541 --> 02:36:53.980
time, the Pinnacles set aside the properties that you see on your screen for,

02:36:54.044 --> 02:36:57.400
again, commercial neighborhood, with the balance of the project into some form of

02:36:57.900 --> 02:37:01.569
residential a different kind of commercial up at the top, if you will, and then

02:37:01.827 --> 02:37:05.677
off to the west or to the left of the image, area for townhouses,

02:37:05.774 --> 02:37:07.368
which has been already reviewed and approved.

02:37:09.454 --> 02:37:12.528
As part of this action, if council's favorable with the idea,

02:37:13.156 --> 02:37:17.470
by association you have also the

02:37:17.970 --> 02:37:21.453
requirement, of course, to amend your future land use map so that

02:37:21.486 --> 02:37:25.286
it harmonizes with the new zoning. And then because this was made part—

02:37:25.786 --> 02:37:28.793
this whole affair was made part of a development agreement set with the master development

02:37:29.293 --> 02:37:32.995
plan, those 2 items also need to be amended. But mainly it's housekeeping. It's just

02:37:33.011 --> 02:37:36.439
simply saying we're okay with this new zoning here,

02:37:36.585 --> 02:37:40.078
this ag zoning, and therefore we're going to amend the master development plan and

02:37:40.578 --> 02:37:43.947
agreement, especially paragraph 3.2, and then our future land use

02:37:44.447 --> 02:37:45.939
map to all fall in line with that decision.

02:37:48.143 --> 02:37:51.640
Any questions? So is the motivation

02:37:51.672 --> 02:37:55.093
for this is to save money on property tax? Yes, and there was some discussion

02:37:55.109 --> 02:37:58.915
at the Planning Commission, who by the way advanced a favorable recommendation on this request,

02:37:59.686 --> 02:38:03.862
as to whether that was going to happen with the zoning of

02:38:04.362 --> 02:38:07.909
the property being changed. And their understanding that everyone walked

02:38:08.409 --> 02:38:12.374
away with was basically, yes, we think that will happen. And the applicant explained the

02:38:12.874 --> 02:38:15.672
why for and how for of that. So assuming they're here tonight, And they can

02:38:16.172 --> 02:38:17.628
also go into detail on that matter if you'd like them to.

02:38:19.850 --> 02:38:23.535
And we haven't had any contact from outside parties or communications, whether by phone

02:38:24.035 --> 02:38:27.090
or email or visitation, that have discussed this matter with us other than just the

02:38:32.748 --> 02:38:36.805
That's it.

02:38:37.209 --> 02:38:38.308
No questions for staff?

02:38:42.843 --> 02:38:45.406
Just making sure there is public hearing, so I can go to that if you're

02:38:45.423 --> 02:38:48.760
okay. We'll go

02:38:49.260 --> 02:38:52.552
ahead and hold the public hearing. It is 8:04, and we'll open

02:38:53.052 --> 02:38:55.878
the public hearing and ask Lacey if she's seen anybody come forward.

02:38:56.183 --> 02:38:59.766
No one. Okay. Is there anyone here who wants to speak on this

02:38:59.878 --> 02:39:03.060
item? Seeing no one,

02:39:03.960 --> 02:39:07.254
I will close the public hearing at 8:04 and bring it back to the council.

02:39:07.334 --> 02:39:11.178
I do know the applicant. The applicant is

02:39:11.292 --> 02:39:14.471
here, and is there any questions for

02:39:14.971 --> 02:39:15.647
the applicant? Anything?

02:39:21.139 --> 02:39:24.377
Any discussion? Anything? I'll share some thoughts.

02:39:25.103 --> 02:39:28.117
I mean, let me just share some thoughts. I'm opposed to this,

02:39:28.891 --> 02:39:33.358
to granting this, and certainly not with any desire

02:39:33.390 --> 02:39:36.799
or willingness to be punitive towards an applicant that wants to reduce their taxes.

02:39:36.847 --> 02:39:39.955
So let me state that publicly for you, Andy, so that you hear that.

02:39:40.797 --> 02:39:42.998
That's not the reason that I'm opposed to this.

02:39:45.549 --> 02:39:48.018
This was part of an MDP, it was part of an MDA, it was part

02:39:48.051 --> 02:39:51.940
of a negotiation to develop this entire area,

02:39:52.606 --> 02:39:56.173
which included giving some multifamily densities in this area.

02:39:57.186 --> 02:40:00.335
And the tradeoff, at least in my mind, was that we were going to ensure

02:40:00.835 --> 02:40:04.328
that there's some commercial development that comes in there along Aviator. I think by choosing

02:40:04.456 --> 02:40:08.224
to move this back to Ag, what we do in a sense is we reset

02:40:08.724 --> 02:40:12.312
the table and we say, come back and petition

02:40:12.812 --> 02:40:15.277
a future council to do whatever you'd like. You can still do that with the

02:40:15.777 --> 02:40:19.589
neighborhood commercial resident designation, zoning designation.

02:40:20.151 --> 02:40:23.741
It's just it requires the council to then understand

02:40:23.773 --> 02:40:26.435
the context of where it's been and why.

02:40:27.236 --> 02:40:30.491
So at least for So for me, that's where I'm feeling some hesitancy to

02:40:30.507 --> 02:40:32.540
want to do this because of that.

02:40:38.628 --> 02:40:42.087
So— Sorry, I was just— Marcus, I just can't remember

02:40:42.249 --> 02:40:45.142
on— as far as greenbelt exemptions,

02:40:45.482 --> 02:40:48.892
you can get greenbelt in any zone, right? So even commercial zone could get a

02:40:49.392 --> 02:40:53.236
greenbelt or an ag exemption as long as there's an agricultural

02:40:53.285 --> 02:40:55.143
use, right? So plant a cherry tree,

02:40:55.159 --> 02:40:58.070
harvest Sell a cherry.

02:40:58.765 --> 02:41:01.337
It's been a minute since I've looked at that code. I think it has to

02:41:01.837 --> 02:41:05.409
be ag-zoned or something similar to that. So I don't think you can greenbelt.

02:41:06.021 --> 02:41:08.854
I know. But I'm not 100% certain on that without researching.

02:41:08.918 --> 02:41:12.830
Residential qualifies for greenbelt, right? So I'd have

02:41:12.862 --> 02:41:15.771
to look at that. I don't know if this is 5 acres or more.

02:41:15.917 --> 02:41:19.437
I don't know how— Right. If it's 5 acres or more, which I don't remember

02:41:19.648 --> 02:41:23.097
the property. Yeah, it's plenty over 5. Plenty over 5,

02:41:23.146 --> 02:41:26.887
okay. Yeah, so I'd have to look at that. I'm not certain. Just a

02:41:27.288 --> 02:41:30.708
thought that there could be some tax savings if there was an avenue

02:41:30.821 --> 02:41:34.096
to go. Go ahead, Greg. I think going

02:41:34.128 --> 02:41:37.259
back to Council Member Wright's point, I think that this was part of a larger

02:41:37.759 --> 02:41:40.679
negotiation with some give and take, and one of those,

02:41:41.434 --> 02:41:45.898
and it landed upon this particular grouping of approvals.

02:41:46.996 --> 02:41:50.568
And so it's sort of, it was taken as a whole when it

02:41:51.068 --> 02:41:54.547
was approved. I will say that when it

02:41:55.047 --> 02:41:58.165
came previously, it was way more dense,

02:41:58.310 --> 02:42:01.619
like the property to the east, and the

02:42:02.021 --> 02:42:05.566
council at the time was the one that was pushing to have

02:42:06.066 --> 02:42:09.643
additional commercial right there. So like, I feel like

02:42:10.114 --> 02:42:13.849
that was an ask of people from our city. As opposed

02:42:14.349 --> 02:42:18.765
to the developer per se. So I'm— I get why they

02:42:19.265 --> 02:42:23.551
would like to reset that piece for tax purposes and really determine

02:42:23.583 --> 02:42:26.699
viability down the road so that when it does come back,

02:42:27.984 --> 02:42:30.940
a future council can look at what the best use of it is.

02:42:31.840 --> 02:42:35.245
I do understand the hesitancy because what

02:42:35.745 --> 02:42:39.907
it does is it puts the responsibility on a future council. To do their homework

02:42:40.407 --> 02:42:43.917
and really understand what's going on with that piece. But I would hope that our

02:42:44.417 --> 02:42:48.603
staff reports have come such a long way from what they used to be that

02:42:48.651 --> 02:42:53.096
they could provide that context in the staff report and

02:42:53.321 --> 02:42:56.687
even older documents to show what it once was.

02:43:02.757 --> 02:43:06.075
But am I allowed to make a comment here? Yeah, I'll let you speak.

02:43:06.155 --> 02:43:08.097
Zap can address the concern. All right, thank you. This is— my name is Andy

02:43:08.597 --> 02:43:12.052
Flamm. I represent represent the property owners. I just

02:43:12.100 --> 02:43:16.138
wanted to say, when this first was negotiated, or I

02:43:16.186 --> 02:43:19.535
guess that's the right word, negotiated and contemplated with the city, it happened over several

02:43:19.632 --> 02:43:23.782
years. And one of the concerns was 800 North in

02:43:23.798 --> 02:43:27.147
Orem. There's a lot of traffic on

02:43:27.179 --> 02:43:30.945
800 North, and there's a lot of old homes that are having to get purchased

02:43:31.445 --> 02:43:34.743
and redeveloped. And forward thinking,

02:43:35.113 --> 02:43:38.573
This was determined in meetings with the Planning Commission and

02:43:38.654 --> 02:43:43.034
staff and others that we wanted to preserve some commercial

02:43:43.534 --> 02:43:45.537
corridor right here in anticipation of a future freeway.

02:43:47.573 --> 02:43:51.836
I don't know when that will be, but it was a good idea and I

02:43:52.336 --> 02:43:54.619
supported it and I thought it was a great compromise.

02:43:55.653 --> 02:43:59.911
I still do. The development agreement— this was actually approved in the

02:43:59.927 --> 02:44:04.240
fall of '21. So the development agreement actually expires

02:44:04.740 --> 02:44:10.621
next year, if I remember right. But so we

02:44:10.686 --> 02:44:14.552
would have to come back if we rezoned this property to

02:44:14.584 --> 02:44:17.980
anything else in the future, and that development agreement probably

02:44:18.060 --> 02:44:21.747
wouldn't even apply at that time anyway. But the reason

02:44:22.329 --> 02:44:25.488
why I'm here trying to rezone it back to ag, which is something you probably

02:44:25.504 --> 02:44:28.914
rarely see, is when we put

02:44:29.414 --> 02:44:32.583
in— Never. Never. When the roads were put in,

02:44:33.020 --> 02:44:36.447
when we had to detain— retain water on our site for Aviator,

02:44:38.475 --> 02:44:42.407
we lost our greenbelt status and we're getting killed. And to

02:44:42.907 --> 02:44:45.823
get it back in, we're told that we need to get it in ag,

02:44:46.290 --> 02:44:50.448
and we're working with some leases to get it back in because right now we've

02:44:50.514 --> 02:44:53.643
had this thing on the market for a year and a half. We've talked

02:44:54.143 --> 02:44:57.594
to a lot of people. There's no demand for

02:44:58.094 --> 02:45:01.483
commercial on a dead-end road at this time. Now, there will be, and we

02:45:01.547 --> 02:45:04.134
believe in that vision of having this be commercial in the future.

02:45:04.681 --> 02:45:07.671
But until then, we're just getting killed on taxes.

02:45:08.505 --> 02:45:12.223
And I know that's been a hot topic, but when you actually look

02:45:12.723 --> 02:45:16.199
at taxes in general, property taxes, but when you

02:45:16.296 --> 02:45:19.983
look at what actually goes to the city from this site, and most

02:45:20.015 --> 02:45:23.936
of it's going to the county or the school district or whatnot. Our application

02:45:23.968 --> 02:45:27.383
fee tonight to even just do this rezone

02:45:27.759 --> 02:45:31.354
probably put the city ahead already.

02:45:33.030 --> 02:45:36.314
So again, we were trying to be— or we were,

02:45:36.765 --> 02:45:40.290
I felt like, a good partner when we brought this project forward. There was a

02:45:40.532 --> 02:45:45.283
lot of discussion and what the city wants, and we were Again,

02:45:45.780 --> 02:45:49.066
we're not asking to change to high-density residential,

02:45:49.098 --> 02:45:52.369
which I've been in so many meetings in here where an applicant comes back

02:45:52.869 --> 02:45:56.248
and says, we tried for a year. Well, this is 6 years in and there's

02:45:56.748 --> 02:45:59.983
still no demand for commercial. And so anyway,

02:46:00.047 --> 02:46:02.933
that's the ask. I just wanted to give a little bit more context to it

02:46:03.975 --> 02:46:07.758
just because I know there's some new faces from when this originally came

02:46:08.258 --> 02:46:11.336
forward. But any other questions for me? from the council or the mayor?

02:46:11.689 --> 02:46:14.948
Go ahead. Yeah, sorry, I looked at the Act and I mean,

02:46:15.880 --> 02:46:19.091
so technically you could get greenbelt status in a commercial zone,

02:46:19.139 --> 02:46:23.009
but it's not a permitted use. So, and it sounds

02:46:23.509 --> 02:46:26.590
like if they lost greenbelt status, it's because they're not engaging in Act uses,

02:46:27.087 --> 02:46:29.239
which means there's no nonconforming use.

02:46:29.929 --> 02:46:33.414
So they couldn't get greenbelt status in a commercial zone

02:46:33.847 --> 02:46:37.235
because it would be a nonconforming use. Unless we made

02:46:37.735 --> 02:46:41.281
some kind of exception or— Well, no, I mean, they're asking to rezone it.

02:46:42.293 --> 02:46:46.628
So then they obviously— ag would be a permitted use. They could get their greenbelt

02:46:47.128 --> 02:46:50.674
status back. We've got the rest of the property fenced and we've got leases.

02:46:51.108 --> 02:46:54.367
And this— it was a dry farm historically, but when

02:46:54.464 --> 02:46:58.382
they came in and they had a grade to put in the retention ponds on

02:46:58.414 --> 02:47:01.770
the 3 different parcels, County just happened

02:47:02.270 --> 02:47:05.703
to come out right when that was all graded, and so anyway, we didn't have

02:47:06.203 --> 02:47:10.215
the new crop out, and we lost that status. So anyway, another question that

02:47:10.296 --> 02:47:13.828
I wonder if we'd consider is the future land use map

02:47:13.908 --> 02:47:15.931
would, with an expired MDA,

02:47:16.863 --> 02:47:20.636
as long as it was on the future land use map as neighborhood

02:47:21.136 --> 02:47:24.329
commercial, would that guide a council to that? And then could

02:47:24.829 --> 02:47:28.423
you write a one-page Agreement would extend it at least six

02:47:28.923 --> 02:47:31.602
years on this piece. It says that it's intended to be,

02:47:32.517 --> 02:47:36.081
you know, because it's going to expire anyway. I mean, all that's

02:47:36.581 --> 02:47:39.982
a possibility. Totally supportive of keeping the the land use

02:47:40.482 --> 02:47:43.996
map with commercial. We're not intending to change that. That's that's

02:47:44.496 --> 02:47:47.833
not what this is about. No, and you know, and my my concern

02:47:48.042 --> 02:47:51.606
again, I'll just restate it. This is a perfect place for someone to

02:47:51.622 --> 02:47:55.010
come back in if it's rezoned. Zone to ag and ask to build multifamily housing

02:47:55.042 --> 02:47:57.858
in this area. And for me, it's just— that's a nonstarter.

02:47:59.812 --> 02:48:02.950
It's intended to be commercial. I understand that perhaps the viability

02:48:03.450 --> 02:48:06.930
of that is not today. But we had this

02:48:07.430 --> 02:48:10.568
conversation, at least I wasn't here at the time, so it predates me.

02:48:10.730 --> 02:48:13.803
2021, I wasn't here. I think you were at

02:48:14.303 --> 02:48:16.344
Planning Commission maybe at the time. I was on Planning Commission at that time.

02:48:16.936 --> 02:48:20.264
And so Look, I've stated my objection.

02:48:20.556 --> 02:48:23.217
The others may feel differently than that. That's where I'm going to land.

02:48:24.840 --> 02:48:30.185
Um, Councilmember Wright, do you feel like the land use designation on the future

02:48:30.685 --> 02:48:33.679
land use map helps to kind of set the vision for this area?

02:48:33.727 --> 02:48:38.058
Because we have lots of areas that we as a city have

02:48:38.558 --> 02:48:41.971
returned to ag because we wanted that to be like the base level,

02:48:42.100 --> 02:48:46.359
and then they have to come and ask for something different. Do I— and

02:48:46.391 --> 02:48:49.281
the reason for that, at least in my mind, is because we wanted to reset

02:48:49.781 --> 02:48:51.995
that so that it would come to something that we want rather than what it

02:48:52.495 --> 02:48:56.330
was. Okay. And for me, commercial makes sense here. It's just—

02:48:56.410 --> 02:48:59.975
and it already has that designation, and it's part of a larger agreement.

02:49:00.039 --> 02:49:02.993
I think that's the— for me, at least, the hurdle that I'm not able to

02:49:03.493 --> 02:49:07.216
get over. What about the expiration of the larger agreement? Because that is something that

02:49:07.730 --> 02:49:11.297
went into place when you were on Planning, and I was— the MDA

02:49:11.797 --> 02:49:14.919
will expire in 6 years, right? We want those things to reset,

02:49:15.518 --> 02:49:19.189
and it's really a question of what does this council want? Does this council want

02:49:19.221 --> 02:49:22.763
to see this maintained as a, as a commercial area, or do we want to

02:49:22.779 --> 02:49:26.111
reset it to, to ag? I've stated my objection to it.

02:49:26.337 --> 02:49:28.804
The rest of you can do what you want. Okay, thank you.

02:49:30.860 --> 02:49:34.115
So even if, even if the future land use map shows commercial, you still feel

02:49:34.615 --> 02:49:37.705
like that there's That they're— There's nothing on the

02:49:38.205 --> 02:49:41.976
future land use map that dictates that it's going to happen. The zoning requires this

02:49:42.476 --> 02:49:45.685
to be commercial now. That's the difference in my mind.

02:49:45.733 --> 02:49:48.334
It's no different than what we dealt with on the north end of the city

02:49:48.383 --> 02:49:52.108
where we have these mixed uses and it says, we'll try to pursue commercial

02:49:52.608 --> 02:49:56.395
uses in this area and it always ends up residential. For me, it's protecting

02:49:56.427 --> 02:49:59.558
some of those commercial uses in an area that I think we're going to want

02:50:00.058 --> 02:50:04.004
and need it. We had an applicant who came before

02:50:04.504 --> 02:50:07.309
us and wanted to build a 12-plex on that commercial lot where Freddy's is at

02:50:07.809 --> 02:50:10.904
now. So I think it's the same argument that I've seen

02:50:11.017 --> 02:50:12.914
time and time again. It's the same movie.

02:50:18.129 --> 02:50:21.566
I'll make a motion, Mayor, if you want, and, and then we'll find

02:50:22.066 --> 02:50:24.310
out where the rest of these guys are at. How does that sound? Do it.

02:50:24.407 --> 02:50:27.520
If you're willing to let me do that, let me get I got to open

02:50:28.020 --> 02:50:31.227
it up here. OK. I have it here.

02:50:31.535 --> 02:50:35.901
I've got it. Mayor, I move that the Eagle Mountain City Council

02:50:36.225 --> 02:50:39.961
deny the reason— the item— what is it? 15B,

02:50:41.013 --> 02:50:44.654
rezoning certain lands known as Pinnacles and approving the Pinnacles Master

02:50:45.154 --> 02:50:49.047
Plan, Master Development Plan, and Master Development Agreement Amendment.

02:50:49.163 --> 02:50:52.352
Second. Okay,

02:50:52.368 --> 02:50:56.375
we have a motion by Councilmember Wright and a second by Councilmember Whiting.

02:50:57.522 --> 02:51:00.965
And I think we were here. Councilmember Whiting? Yes.

02:51:01.482 --> 02:51:04.652
Councilmember Hewish? Yes. Councilmember Clark? No.

02:51:05.057 --> 02:51:07.918
Councilmember Wright? Yes. Okay, that passes 3-1.

02:51:08.693 --> 02:51:12.245
Uh, thank you. Let's see, that takes us

02:51:12.745 --> 02:51:16.616
to item 16A, uh, an ordinance of Eagle Mountain City enacting

02:51:16.650 --> 02:51:20.128
Chapter 3.50 of Eagle Mountain Municipal

02:51:20.628 --> 02:51:22.328
Code establishing property tax allocations.

02:51:23.790 --> 02:51:27.499
Actually, before I move on, I don't think this was a big

02:51:27.999 --> 02:51:31.835
deal, but I— when there's one vote, did you want to make any— do

02:51:32.335 --> 02:51:35.786
I need to stop or— Oh, I think

02:51:36.286 --> 02:51:38.821
it really just comes down to the fact that I think that it is a

02:51:38.901 --> 02:51:42.482
good move to reset that land with the expectation

02:51:42.514 --> 02:51:46.401
that it's commercial because it's in our future land use

02:51:46.901 --> 02:51:48.977
map. And I remember the ask was something that the city asked the landowner of,

02:51:49.025 --> 02:51:52.454
so that's why I just felt like it, it made sense to

02:51:52.954 --> 02:51:56.252
approve it. But I respect everyone else's right to make their

02:51:56.300 --> 02:52:00.402
choices too. Yep. I just— before, I like

02:52:00.902 --> 02:52:04.152
everybody to have a chance to explain if they choose. So, uh, yeah,

02:52:04.216 --> 02:52:07.678
it's, uh, enacting Chapter 3.15 or 3.50 of the

02:52:08.159 --> 02:52:11.343
Eagle Mountain Municipal Code establishing property tax allocations.

02:52:12.410 --> 02:52:15.143
Um, I, uh, Evan's got some information,

02:52:15.417 --> 02:52:18.707
so. Yes,

02:52:18.867 --> 02:52:23.062
uh, Mayor and Council, we'll run through this code just to kind of COVID

02:52:23.562 --> 02:52:26.148
what's, uh, changed since the last time we talked about it and, uh,

02:52:26.758 --> 02:52:30.420
answer any remaining questions. And I will say right off the bat

02:52:30.920 --> 02:52:34.284
that, uh, this is definitely not a perfect code. Uh, it's something that

02:52:34.784 --> 02:52:38.616
we haven't had before, as was mentioned, And it's the best shot

02:52:39.116 --> 02:52:42.190
we're giving it right now. But I expect there'll be some kinks in it that

02:52:42.690 --> 02:52:46.933
we'll probably have to work out later as we go through this process. So we'll

02:52:47.433 --> 02:52:50.533
work on this together over the next budget season and see how it goes

02:52:51.433 --> 02:52:54.020
and modify as needed. But we tried to cover everything we could with it.

02:52:55.850 --> 02:52:59.520
So as you recall, the instructions that we received from the council

02:52:59.862 --> 02:53:03.773
is to draft this code. That would essentially allocate the

02:53:03.966 --> 02:53:07.676
property taxes, all the property taxes that we receive to

02:53:08.176 --> 02:53:11.691
public safety into a public safety fund that would be restricted

02:53:12.191 --> 02:53:15.594
for those uses. And then it would establish certain coverage

02:53:15.611 --> 02:53:20.204
ratios that we'd then be monitoring from year to year to make sure that where

02:53:20.704 --> 02:53:23.513
we need to reduce property taxes 'cause we're collecting too much, we do that.

02:53:23.577 --> 02:53:26.324
Where we need to increase property taxes 'cause we don't have enough, we do that.

02:53:27.352 --> 02:53:30.641
But then throughout the course of the year, we're also keeping tab on the certain

02:53:31.141 --> 02:53:34.924
metrics and, you know, different analysis to make sure that we have a good handle

02:53:35.116 --> 02:53:38.389
on where our public safety needs are and that we are tracking

02:53:38.421 --> 02:53:42.031
this effectively and transparently for the public

02:53:42.448 --> 02:53:46.218
so that this is working as intended. So just to break

02:53:46.718 --> 02:53:50.919
it down a little bit for you, and I'll just run through this fairly

02:53:51.419 --> 02:53:54.739
quickly, the first couple of pages They go through

02:53:55.239 --> 02:53:58.793
the purpose and the general policy, and so you have just essentially

02:53:58.939 --> 02:54:01.765
breaking down that what we just said. This is, you know,

02:54:01.781 --> 02:54:04.471
for property tax to go into this public safety fund.

02:54:05.964 --> 02:54:08.821
But I want to call attention to a couple of things in here.

02:54:09.677 --> 02:54:13.196
First, there was, you know, commentary made last time

02:54:13.309 --> 02:54:16.885
just about how do we make sure that this this code isn't

02:54:17.385 --> 02:54:19.657
just something that we just have, and it's not. I don't want to use the

02:54:20.157 --> 02:54:23.171
word necessarily enforced, but we talk about accountability, trying to make sure that it's something

02:54:23.187 --> 02:54:26.861
that we are sticking to, that we're not just kind of loosely

02:54:26.893 --> 02:54:30.342
adhering to. And so I was trying to come up with some

02:54:30.842 --> 02:54:33.808
method that we could do that, some sort of pressure that it puts both on

02:54:34.308 --> 02:54:37.706
council and staff to make sure that we

02:54:37.979 --> 02:54:41.589
move forward with this vision, but it's also not

02:54:41.733 --> 02:54:45.204
too heavily impactful that we start kicking ourselves because

02:54:45.704 --> 02:54:49.348
of how we're doing this. And so what I came up with for now

02:54:49.558 --> 02:54:52.925
is that essentially we're creating an order of operations

02:54:53.023 --> 02:54:56.867
with things wherein we're forcing

02:54:57.543 --> 02:55:01.018
the conversation of the public safety side to come earlier or

02:55:01.307 --> 02:55:04.670
as early as possible in this, where obviously we know as a

02:55:05.170 --> 02:55:08.662
growing city, one of the most important things that we have to monitor is our

02:55:08.759 --> 02:55:12.547
own staffing levels citywide. And so we know

02:55:13.047 --> 02:55:16.943
there's going to be questions every year about how much staff that we can allocate

02:55:17.745 --> 02:55:21.018
for all sorts of city services. But when we're talking

02:55:21.050 --> 02:55:24.804
about property tax as kind of that key revenue source that's going to fund public

02:55:25.304 --> 02:55:28.558
safety, and with this coverage ratio, knowing that that coverage

02:55:28.574 --> 02:55:32.376
ratio may require more sales tax or less sales tax from

02:55:32.424 --> 02:55:36.194
year to year, we need to settle that core public service

02:55:36.694 --> 02:55:40.462
that we offer as a city, um, first and then talk about the

02:55:40.687 --> 02:55:44.699
other staffing stuff later. And when I say later, it's not that finance

02:55:45.199 --> 02:55:48.471
can't necessarily start gathering some of that information, start getting

02:55:48.503 --> 02:55:52.114
some of that feedback so that we can continue the process as quickly as it

02:55:52.614 --> 02:55:56.463
needs to go, but no decisions necessarily by the city council

02:55:56.495 --> 02:56:00.507
or their staff will happen with the other staff until the

02:56:01.007 --> 02:56:04.138
public safety fund and everything like that has been figured out. If that, hopefully that

02:56:04.638 --> 02:56:07.801
makes sense. I also wanted to just

02:56:08.301 --> 02:56:11.820
call out an attention, just there's obviously on page 2, there's some definitions.

02:56:12.417 --> 02:56:15.612
One of the ones that's another change

02:56:15.790 --> 02:56:20.010
that I didn't really notate specific on here is that there's a definition

02:56:20.510 --> 02:56:24.038
for qualified public safety costs. I added in

02:56:24.087 --> 02:56:29.462
there that some language that makes it clear that facilities falls

02:56:29.495 --> 02:56:32.956
under that as well. So we can make sure that we're— we do

02:56:33.456 --> 02:56:36.465
have a public safety impact fund already, impact fee fund,

02:56:37.302 --> 02:56:40.762
but if there's other revenues we could pull off property tax to help pay debt

02:56:41.262 --> 02:56:44.475
service and other things for public safety facilities, then that's worked in

02:56:44.508 --> 02:56:47.153
there as well. Um,

02:56:47.867 --> 02:56:51.827
on pages 3 and 4 talks about the creation of that public safety fund.

02:56:52.509 --> 02:56:55.407
I did eliminate a sentence that as I went,

02:56:55.826 --> 02:56:59.851
so there is specific state code that talks about the creation of funds

02:56:59.980 --> 02:57:03.120
for property tax dollars. And so there was

02:57:03.620 --> 02:57:07.200
a line that we had in there that I felt like ran contrary to

02:57:07.410 --> 02:57:11.168
what state code has in there. It doesn't make any meaningful change to

02:57:11.459 --> 02:57:13.750
the code itself that we have in here. So this is just more of a

02:57:14.250 --> 02:57:17.433
cleanup item. The rest of that,

02:57:18.474 --> 02:57:22.303
there's some stipulations about making sure that the funds can't

02:57:22.803 --> 02:57:25.543
just be transferred to and fro for whatever reasons. It needs to stay in the

02:57:26.043 --> 02:57:27.859
public safety fund for those public safety purposes.

02:57:29.322 --> 02:57:32.874
The coverage ratio evaluations added some

02:57:33.374 --> 02:57:36.926
language in there about making

02:57:37.426 --> 02:57:41.576
sure that we're collecting some level of service metrics, and those level of service metrics

02:57:42.076 --> 02:57:45.930
are gonna be what helps us determine what

02:57:46.430 --> 02:57:49.711
is that public safety funding requirement We're not just throwing

02:57:49.889 --> 02:57:53.554
out feel-good numbers of how many sheriff's deputies or whatnot

02:57:54.054 --> 02:57:55.548
that we need. It has to be supported by evidence.

02:57:58.746 --> 02:58:02.506
Pages 5 and 6 goes into additional guidance

02:58:03.006 --> 02:58:06.668
on the fund balance, how we handle when we have an increasing or decreasing

02:58:06.701 --> 02:58:10.095
fund balance, how do we handle other cost allocations,

02:58:10.144 --> 02:58:13.796
which is mostly relevant if the city chooses at

02:58:14.296 --> 02:58:18.142
some point to establish its own independent police department will wanna handle things like

02:58:18.929 --> 02:58:22.218
HR costs and IT costs and risk management and things like that that

02:58:22.298 --> 02:58:25.410
often fall in other departments but then have to get transferred to and from.

02:58:26.469 --> 02:58:29.742
And then there's the annual reporting side of things.

02:58:30.560 --> 02:58:33.721
And with the annual reporting, I did add a

02:58:34.221 --> 02:58:37.475
section in there about making sure that annual reporting or

02:58:37.975 --> 02:58:41.621
an annual summary makes it to the public. Earlier in the process

02:58:42.008 --> 02:58:45.879
so that it's not something where the public's only finding out about it at the

02:58:46.379 --> 02:58:49.651
tail end of the budgeting process. That just as much as you are

02:58:49.683 --> 02:58:53.179
also receiving that information about the public safety fund

02:58:53.679 --> 02:58:56.950
and the public safety needs in our city, the public is also learning about that

02:58:57.450 --> 02:59:00.613
through this, you know, similar metrics and things like that and receiving it in

02:59:00.645 --> 02:59:01.459
multiple formats.

02:59:05.184 --> 02:59:08.068
Besides that, I do want to pass some time to Kimberly.

02:59:09.102 --> 02:59:13.694
We talked about this a fair bit and just wanted to give

02:59:13.743 --> 02:59:16.986
her some time to kind of talk a little bit about her thoughts

02:59:17.486 --> 02:59:21.321
on just how to implement this. And then just some things that would help if

02:59:21.450 --> 02:59:25.079
you guys can work together on making sure that the process keeps

02:59:25.579 --> 02:59:27.744
moving smoothly as we go through this. So.

02:59:37.759 --> 02:59:41.528
So, Mayor and Council, a couple of items just to

02:59:41.592 --> 02:59:44.912
kind of keep in mind with this particular code.

02:59:44.976 --> 02:59:48.232
So one of the items is the special revenue

02:59:48.248 --> 02:59:51.359
fund that we're creating. It will be created from a budgetary purpose,

02:59:52.049 --> 02:59:55.849
so it it'll work similar to the way that we have

02:59:56.186 --> 03:00:00.035
our water and sewer impact fees. So the water

03:00:00.535 --> 03:00:03.408
and sewer Sewer impact fees we separate from a budgetary standpoint,

03:00:03.745 --> 03:00:07.279
but in the year-end financial statements those are actually just part of the water fund

03:00:07.779 --> 03:00:12.098
and the sewer fund because you can't have a special revenue fund for non-government

03:00:12.598 --> 03:00:16.146
activities. Okay, so this there's some accounting

03:00:16.258 --> 03:00:20.338
provisions where it's like state code we can do certain things,

03:00:20.354 --> 03:00:24.065
but when it comes to the financial statements for accounting, this won't

03:00:24.565 --> 03:00:28.614
meet the requirements for an like a separate special revenue fund. So that's

03:00:28.663 --> 03:00:31.672
all on our books and the things that we're doing. You'll see it as a

03:00:32.172 --> 03:00:35.521
separate fund. We'll budget it separately. But in the year-end financial statements,

03:00:35.554 --> 03:00:37.613
it will be grouped up as part of the general fund.

03:00:39.399 --> 03:00:41.607
So I just want to make sure that that part is clear.

03:00:43.353 --> 03:00:47.012
Then that when we talk about trying

03:00:47.141 --> 03:00:50.413
to ensure that decisions have

03:00:50.445 --> 03:00:53.947
been made kind of about the funding for public safety before we

03:00:54.447 --> 03:00:57.945
make decisions on other things. What those decisions that

03:00:58.445 --> 03:01:01.959
we're talking about from the council's perspective is how

03:01:02.459 --> 03:01:05.941
much revenue do we need to be able to generate from a property

03:01:05.973 --> 03:01:09.313
tax standpoint compared to other revenue resources

03:01:09.971 --> 03:01:13.568
in relation to funding public safety with the understanding

03:01:13.680 --> 03:01:17.004
that we won't actually receive a certified tax rate until June,

03:01:17.293 --> 03:01:20.713
which is way past when we have a tentative budget that has to come to

03:01:20.745 --> 03:01:23.924
the council. So when we're talking about making these

03:01:24.101 --> 03:01:28.083
decisions for the planning and moving things forward, it's not

03:01:28.131 --> 03:01:31.969
something that can wait until the certified tax rate is available.

03:01:32.804 --> 03:01:36.834
So in Utah, when we're talking about taxes, it really doesn't have anything

03:01:36.882 --> 03:01:40.302
to do with the rate. The rate is just what happens based

03:01:40.318 --> 03:01:43.642
on the revenue you decide that you need. Thank you. And that is how

03:01:44.142 --> 03:01:47.530
it's designed to work. So it will be looking

03:01:47.642 --> 03:01:50.951
at that and that we will have projections from

03:01:50.999 --> 03:01:53.810
that. And it doesn't prevent you when it gets to the end and you get

03:01:54.310 --> 03:01:57.569
your certified rate and the revenue you thought you were going to get would

03:01:57.986 --> 03:02:01.906
be looking at truth in taxation. It means that we want

03:02:02.406 --> 03:02:06.564
to get mentally at the perspective of is the council is considering truth in taxation

03:02:06.741 --> 03:02:09.918
every year. It doesn't mean you end up having to go through Truth in Taxation.

03:02:10.447 --> 03:02:13.525
Your decision is how much tax revenue do you need

03:02:13.894 --> 03:02:17.854
to fund this part of the service. So in terms of the

03:02:18.303 --> 03:02:20.693
order and the timeline of things, I want to make sure we have a chance

03:02:21.193 --> 03:02:23.755
to talk about that and what that looks like.

03:02:24.749 --> 03:02:27.908
Do you have any questions kind of about what I'm, you know,

03:02:27.924 --> 03:02:29.624
kind of trying to cover from that perspective?

03:02:30.666 --> 03:02:34.370
So just so we're all on the same page,

03:02:34.562 --> 03:02:38.108
this is kind of what I hoped we would have had earlier in

03:02:38.608 --> 03:02:41.863
the year. Uh, I think our coverage ratio was 32% or

03:02:42.200 --> 03:02:46.117
35% going into this whole conversation, and we started

03:02:46.617 --> 03:02:49.969
out, let's go 100% coverage, and now we've kind of dialed it

03:02:50.469 --> 03:02:54.061
back. I think we'll be at 52% with— for this

03:02:54.561 --> 03:02:59.021
year. So going into next year, as we're having conversations

03:02:59.037 --> 03:03:02.503
with Chief Knutson and staffing and going through those metrics,

03:03:03.929 --> 03:03:08.399
um, is it possible for us to kind of anticipate

03:03:08.415 --> 03:03:12.181
what that revenue might look like from a property tax perspective and

03:03:12.197 --> 03:03:15.642
match that up? Like, that, that, that's really the goal, is just

03:03:16.142 --> 03:03:19.039
that right out of the gate we're looking at the coverage ratio, we're looking at

03:03:19.905 --> 03:03:23.767
that number one. And Chief Knudson is going to, um,

03:03:25.000 --> 03:03:28.867
you know, seeing what we've just kind of went through as a city He's

03:03:29.367 --> 03:03:32.182
going to be probably pretty measured in, okay, this is the ask, and as a

03:03:32.682 --> 03:03:35.595
council, we're going to say this is safety,

03:03:35.756 --> 03:03:38.959
and residents now will know that every dollar they're

03:03:39.459 --> 03:03:43.164
paying in property tax goes into the safety fund. So I just

03:03:43.664 --> 03:03:45.833
want to make sure that we're logistically going to be able to do that,

03:03:45.849 --> 03:03:49.609
understanding that the certified tax rate won't really be established until

03:03:51.656 --> 03:03:55.025
later, later in the process, but we'll at least know going into that.

03:03:55.462 --> 03:03:59.164
If we need to increase a little bit to get another half

03:03:59.664 --> 03:04:03.154
a million dollars or decrease because the data center

03:04:03.234 --> 03:04:06.744
property tax has taken us way over what the need is for sheriffs, you know,

03:04:06.760 --> 03:04:10.269
and we might need to dial that back a little bit. Right.

03:04:10.445 --> 03:04:14.035
So it's tying these 2 policies together. The policy

03:04:14.051 --> 03:04:17.993
where you said what is the coverage ratio that you want to cover,

03:04:19.323 --> 03:04:23.809
and if you're trying to target a minimum of 75% to

03:04:23.825 --> 03:04:27.547
that, then that really is where you are coming up

03:04:28.047 --> 03:04:31.586
with this is what the existing contract is, and then

03:04:32.086 --> 03:04:35.007
we start considering asks or what those staffing needs are,

03:04:35.623 --> 03:04:40.006
and then being able to say to cover 75% of that, we are talking revenue

03:04:40.105 --> 03:04:41.277
at least at this number.

03:04:44.009 --> 03:04:48.093
You agree that is all possible early in the budget process? Absolutely. possible early

03:04:48.109 --> 03:04:51.483
in the budget. Yeah. What it can't be or has to happen

03:04:51.983 --> 03:04:55.114
is very early on in the budget you have to say regardless we don't

03:04:55.614 --> 03:04:58.649
care about the 75% policy, we don't want to consider tax,

03:04:59.115 --> 03:05:02.715
truth in taxation at all this year, right. So you're either

03:05:02.891 --> 03:05:06.571
using the policy to drive you, it's not, you're not deciding

03:05:06.603 --> 03:05:10.588
whether you're doing truth in taxation. You're deciding whether you're following your policy

03:05:11.182 --> 03:05:14.732
and the certified tax rate when it comes in The revenue you said you

03:05:15.232 --> 03:05:18.618
needed, it either falls within that line or it just means you now have

03:05:18.634 --> 03:05:21.508
these public hearings that you do. Or, Kimberly,

03:05:21.941 --> 03:05:25.521
perhaps there's something that you know is coming online that

03:05:25.618 --> 03:05:28.989
isn't in the diagnostic or in the report so

03:05:29.489 --> 03:05:32.650
that you can look at that and say, okay, we are 3 months from this

03:05:32.778 --> 03:05:36.856
becoming a reality, right? I like this,

03:05:36.872 --> 03:05:41.480
Councilmember Herwish, because it creates an accountability for

03:05:41.980 --> 03:05:44.417
council members to be aware of that metrics.

03:05:45.444 --> 03:05:48.349
And I mean, I've been here a number of years and this is the first

03:05:48.849 --> 03:05:52.233
time I've ever really looked at it from that lens. And that's why I'm grateful

03:05:52.265 --> 03:05:55.603
to Councilmember Hewish for kind of pushing me in that

03:05:55.652 --> 03:05:58.669
direction to examine it. And I, I also,

03:05:58.701 --> 03:06:02.104
I'm going to say this, it's kind of a moving target. That's what you're saying

03:06:02.216 --> 03:06:05.812
is it's a moving target. And, and we might think it's X, But it really

03:06:05.844 --> 03:06:08.466
is going to be Y, and it can be up or it can be down,

03:06:08.803 --> 03:06:12.003
right? Usually it goes up, doesn't usually

03:06:12.036 --> 03:06:14.688
go down, or it's just flat. So,

03:06:16.117 --> 03:06:19.411
but I like that it compels us to evaluate that and to say, okay,

03:06:19.491 --> 03:06:22.544
we're still in a good range, or boy, we're way out of whack on our

03:06:23.044 --> 03:06:25.969
range. We need to talk about how do we, how do we fill the gap.

03:06:28.104 --> 03:06:31.818
The other thing that it— I'm just thinking about incentive

03:06:32.318 --> 03:06:35.438
structures as a council. We're going to want more commercial. Because we know when we

03:06:35.535 --> 03:06:39.709
add more rooftops, we need more sheriffs, more cost.

03:06:39.854 --> 03:06:43.146
Truth in taxation, that's not fun. At least I didn't think it

03:06:43.646 --> 03:06:47.577
was very much fun. Commercial actually brings more calls for service

03:06:48.077 --> 03:06:51.511
than residential, though. Well, I guess depending on the commercial, right?

03:06:51.672 --> 03:06:54.594
Like the data centers probably aren't getting too many calls. Correct.

03:06:54.722 --> 03:06:57.709
So I have a clarifying question, and I know you and I have talked about

03:06:58.209 --> 03:07:01.545
this a few times, and sometimes we don't always say things the same way,

03:07:01.561 --> 03:07:04.880
so I'm going to make sure that staff and you are on the same page.

03:07:05.393 --> 03:07:08.696
Because you said things like, this is a public safety fund.

03:07:08.744 --> 03:07:11.887
I want to identify that. And then you said we only covered it at 32%.

03:07:12.192 --> 03:07:15.816
Knowing where we're headed, is this only for

03:07:16.316 --> 03:07:20.146
the sheriffs to evaluate, or is it all? Because we are way over 32% if

03:07:20.646 --> 03:07:23.914
it's for both. So I think it's unfair to say— Yep. Okay,

03:07:23.994 --> 03:07:28.766
okay. So, so I want to make sure that now we're bringing in next Yes,

03:07:28.830 --> 03:07:32.433
so is this public safety fund? You're not even necessarily bringing in that.

03:07:32.514 --> 03:07:36.423
We already have a public safety division that

03:07:36.921 --> 03:07:40.208
includes more than just the contract, right? We have dispatch fees that we pay.

03:07:40.419 --> 03:07:44.076
We have emergency management services. So yes,

03:07:44.188 --> 03:07:47.636
that's a clarification. Is this only the

03:07:48.136 --> 03:07:50.407
sheriffs? We have school crossings. Or is this public safety?

03:07:52.253 --> 03:07:56.026
School crossing guards. We're talking police and fire. Like, are we trying to meet 75%

03:07:56.042 --> 03:07:59.462
of what? What? And 32% is not the

03:07:59.962 --> 03:08:02.914
correct number because we're paying more than that now out of property tax because it's

03:08:03.414 --> 03:08:07.136
100% covered for the, for fire out of property tax currently.

03:08:08.132 --> 03:08:11.343
Well, now you're bringing in fire, which will be— is it

03:08:11.843 --> 03:08:15.245
part of this fund or not? It would be, yeah. I mean, I don't know

03:08:15.261 --> 03:08:18.520
that it's drafted that way today, but next year

03:08:18.969 --> 03:08:22.967
we could amend the code to include fire. Right now it's just specifically the sheriff's

03:08:22.983 --> 03:08:26.644
contract. But next year— It's both. It's public safety.

03:08:26.901 --> 03:08:29.454
That's what I'm asking.

03:08:30.289 --> 03:08:33.628
The code as it's written right now specifies police services.

03:08:34.302 --> 03:08:37.835
Even though it says public safety fund, it is written right

03:08:38.335 --> 03:08:43.695
now as police services, but with the idea in mind that it could be amended

03:08:44.195 --> 03:08:47.372
next year to then keep the title as public safety,

03:08:47.468 --> 03:08:50.461
but then change the the scope to include fire.

03:08:50.864 --> 03:08:53.425
So why don't we just write it for both now? Is the intent not to

03:08:53.925 --> 03:08:57.839
be for both? Well, because we just don't have fire today, but we

03:08:58.339 --> 03:09:01.710
could amend it. It can still— even if we're paying 100%

03:09:02.210 --> 03:09:05.001
of fire and 50% of— that's still an 85% coverage out of property tax.

03:09:05.743 --> 03:09:08.222
If it's legal to put that in the code, I'd be okay with that.

03:09:08.271 --> 03:09:09.947
They're both contracts. What's the difference?

03:09:12.363 --> 03:09:16.439
That's up to you. That's why I was asking. I think— I mean, it's Yeah,

03:09:16.519 --> 03:09:20.051
I agree. I think we were just focused more on the sheriff's contract, and then

03:09:20.551 --> 03:09:24.209
next year, once we actually have fire announced,

03:09:24.305 --> 03:09:27.468
then we amend the code to include— Would you be okay to just do public

03:09:27.968 --> 03:09:32.027
safety? Oh, I think absolutely. There's no reason to bring this back. Yeah, I think

03:09:32.252 --> 03:09:37.534
the only issue would just come to, you know, when we're talking about staffing

03:09:37.646 --> 03:09:41.226
and coverage and things like that, we obviously just can't talk about that really

03:09:41.242 --> 03:09:44.970
until we know what with UFA once we actually have that under

03:09:45.470 --> 03:09:49.206
a contract. Property taxes are covering that. I think it's unfair to say that 100

03:09:49.336 --> 03:09:54.205
plus 85, that's more than— I think that's more than what

03:09:54.705 --> 03:09:58.154
I— that's my opinion. That's an opinion. That's not— Yeah, I think the weighted

03:09:58.219 --> 03:10:02.982
average of where we landed with the sheriff's contract, the 69%

03:10:03.015 --> 03:10:06.827
increase and 100 of UFSA, when you do the weighted average,

03:10:06.843 --> 03:10:10.552
it's like 77. 86%. Which is why I say it still falls within that.

03:10:10.777 --> 03:10:14.295
It would, it would, yep. So we don't need 85% of

03:10:14.391 --> 03:10:17.251
just the sheriffs, we need 85% of all public safety? Correct.

03:10:17.363 --> 03:10:21.106
Regardless of whether we're in-house or through UFSA?

03:10:21.540 --> 03:10:25.042
Correct. Is that, is that clear?

03:10:25.315 --> 03:10:28.608
Can I— let me state this, Mayor, because I mean, I think we're going

03:10:29.108 --> 03:10:32.207
